var e={"geriatrics-ethics-cga":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**CGA** is a multidimensional, interdisciplinary diagnostic and treatment process for a frail older person.`,`It covers medical, functional, psychological, social and environmental status.`,`It ends in **one problem list, one plan, one responsible clinician**.`,`CGA is a process, not a questionnaire.`,`Ward-based CGA keeps more patients **alive and at home at 12 months** (Cochrane 2017).`,`Advice-only consultation teams that do not act show little benefit.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Target frailty, not age**: 60 or over with a geriatric giant, 5 or more drugs, or recent discharge.`,`**Clinical Frailty Scale 5 or more** triggers full CGA; CFS 4 needs a targeted review.`,`Score the CFS on the patient's status 2 weeks before the acute illness.`,`**Geriatric giants (Isaacs)**: immobility, instability, incontinence, impaired intellect, plus iatrogenesis.`,`**5 Ms**: Mind, Mobility, Medications, Multicomplexity and Matters Most.`,`**WHO ICOPE** screens six domains: cognition, mobility, nutrition, vision, hearing and mood.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Senescent cells release IL-6 and TNF-alpha, causing low-grade **inflammageing**.`,`Each organ keeps resting function but loses reserve; this is **homeostenosis**.`,`A small stress such as a UTI or new drug tips the weakest system over.`,`The symptom (fall, confusion) is often far from the diseased organ: **atypical presentation**.`,`GFR falls about 1 mL/min a year after 40, but **creatinine stays normal** as muscle falls.`,`Fever and leucocytosis are blunted, so pneumonia may present as confusion or a fall.`,`So in old age **function is the most sensitive vital sign**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask what the person could do **6 months ago and now**; ADL lost in weeks is disease.`,`**IADL loss** (money, medicines, transport) is the earliest marker of dementia.`,`Ask the three **STEADI** questions; one yes triggers a falls assessment.`,`Do a **brown-bag review** of every strip, tonic, ayurvedic and homeopathic remedy.`,`Take memory history from an informant, and ask about continence, weight and teeth.`,`**Interview the patient alone** and ask who controls the money.`,`Ask the carer about coping, and ask the patient **what matters most**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Watch the patient rise and walk** before using the stethoscope.`,`**Timed Up and Go 12 seconds or more** means increased fall risk.`,`Measure **lying and standing BP**; a fall of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic is orthostatic hypotension.`,`Grip under **28 kg in men and 18 kg in women** at 65 or over is low strength (AWGS 2025).`,`Record weight and calf circumference; under 34 cm (men) or 33 cm (women) suggests low muscle.`,`Check vision, whispered-voice hearing, mouth, dentures, feet and the sacrum.`,`Screen cognition with **Mini-Cog, HMSE or vernacular MoCA**, and mood with **GDS-15**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`There is **no 'geriatric profile'**; order tests that answer questions raised by the CGA.`,`CBC, sodium and potassium, glucose or HbA1c, TSH and vitamin B12.`,`Always calculate **Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance** before dosing renally cleared drugs.`,`Check 25-OH vitamin D when falls or fracture risk are being addressed.`,`Do urinalysis **only with urinary symptoms or fever**; asymptomatic bacteriuria needs no treatment.`,`Do an **ECG before donepezil**, tricyclics or QT-prolonging drugs, and in falls or syncope.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Write **one problem list** ranked by what matters most to the patient.`,`Set a **functional goal** for each problem, such as reaching the toilet unaided at night.`,`Spread the CGA over **2-3 visits**: drugs and mobility, then cognition and mood, then social.`,`Prescribe **resistance and balance exercise 2-3 times a week**.`,`Advise protein **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day** spread over meals.`,`Correct senses and teeth; aids are free for BPL seniors under **Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana**.`,`Make the home safe with grab bars, night lights and no loose mats.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Start low, go slow, but go**: do not under-treat because of age alone.`,`Stop drugs with no indication and avoid prescribing cascades (Beers 2023, STOPP/START v3).`,`Frail diabetics: **HbA1c under 8.0%** in complex health; avoid hypoglycaemia (ADA 2026).`,`Aim for systolic BP about **130-150 mmHg** if standing BP falls.`,`Vaccinate: yearly influenza, **pneumococcal from 50**, recombinant zoster from 50, Td every 10 years.`,`Give one **RSV dose at 75 or over**, or at 50-74 if at increased risk.`,`Start an early **advance care planning** conversation while capacity is intact.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Acute confusion is delirium** until proved otherwise; search the same day.`,`Loss of a basic ADL that was intact last month is never just ageing.`,`Weight loss over **5% in 6 months** needs investigation.`,`Head injury on an anticoagulant needs a **same-day CT** even if the patient looks well.`,`A carer saying 'I cannot cope' is a social emergency.`,`Unexplained bruises, burns or poor hygiene suggest abuse; interview alone.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review in **4-12 weeks**, sooner for the frailer; repeat weight, TUG, ADL, GDS-15 and drug list.`,`See every patient **within 1-2 weeks of discharge** and reconcile medicines.`,`Refer to the **NPHCE** district geriatric clinic or a memory clinic for complex cases.`,`Link to **PM-JAY Vay Vandana**: Rs 5 lakh a year for everyone 70 or over.`,`Give **Elderline 14567** to every isolated older person.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs commonly started after CGA`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU oral weekly`,`8 weeks, then 800-2000 IU daily`,`Avoid huge annual boluses; they increase falls`],[`Calcium carbonate`,`500 mg elemental oral once or twice daily`,`Long term, if diet poor`,`Constipation; separate from levothyroxine and iron`],[`Sertraline`,`25 mg oral morning, then 50 mg (max 200)`,`12 months after remission`,`Hyponatraemia (check Na at 2-4 weeks), GI bleed`],[`Escitalopram`,`5 mg oral daily, max 10 mg over 65`,`12 months after remission`,`QT prolongation, hyponatraemia`],[`Mirtazapine`,`7.5-15 mg oral at night (max 45)`,`As for depression`,`Sedation, weight gain`],[`Donepezil`,`5 mg at night, then 10 mg after 4-6 weeks`,`While benefit continues`,`Bradycardia, heart block; pulse and ECG first`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g every 6 hours`,`Regular for chronic pain`,`Max 3 g/day if frail or under 50 kg`],[`Macrogol`,`13 g oral once or twice daily`,`While on opioids or iron`,`Bloating; needs fluids`],[`Oral nutritional supplement`,`400 kcal and 30 g protein daily`,`At least 1 month`,`Give between meals, not instead`]]},{heading:`CGA screening tools and cut-offs`,columns:[`Domain`,`Tool`,`Abnormal`,`Watch out`],rows:[[`Basic ADL`,`Katz (0-6)`,`4 moderate; 2 or less severe`,`Does not test IADLs`],[`Instrumental ADL`,`Lawton (0-8)`,`Any fall from baseline`,`Earliest dementia marker`],[`Rehabilitation`,`Barthel (0-100)`,`Under 60 dependent`,`Tracks stroke and hip-fracture rehab`],[`Cognition`,`Mini-Cog / MoCA`,`Mini-Cog 2 or less; MoCA under 26`,`MMSE penalises the unschooled`],[`Mood`,`GDS-15`,`5 or more suggests depression`,`Better than PHQ-9 in the ill`],[`Nutrition`,`MNA-SF (0-14)`,`0-7 malnourished; 8-11 at risk`,`Weight loss over 5% counts anyway`],[`Mobility`,`Timed Up and Go`,`12 s or more`,`Over 20 s suggests dependence`],[`Frailty`,`Clinical Frailty Scale (1-9)`,`5 or more frail`,`Score status 2 weeks before illness`]]}],remember:[`5 Ms: Mind, Mobility, Medications, Multicomplexity, Matters Most.`,`Function is the vital sign: what could they do 6 months ago?`,`Serum creatinine lies in the old; calculate creatinine clearance.`,`Watch them walk before the stethoscope; TUG 12 s or more = fall risk.`,`Never use an unadjusted MMSE in an unschooled patient; use HMSE.`,`Interview the elder alone for part of every visit.`,`Target frailty, not age: CFS 5 or more gets full CGA.`]},updated:[`AWGS 2025: grip strength under 28 kg (men) / 18 kg (women) at 65 or over and under 34 / 20 kg at 50-64; low strength plus low mass makes sarcopenia`,`AWGS 2025: gait speed moved out of the sarcopenia diagnosis to an outcome measure (AWGS 2019 under 1.0 m/s over 6 m kept for reference)`,`ACIP 2025: pneumococcal vaccine from 50 years (PCV20 or PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23); single RSV dose at 75+ and at 50-74 if at increased risk (was 60-74)`,`ADA 2026: frail older adults - HbA1c under 8.0% in complex/intermediate health and avoid reliance on HbA1c in very complex/poor health (replaces 7.5-8.5%)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Comprehensive geriatric assessment`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Multidimensional, interdisciplinary diagnostic and treatment process** for a frail older person - medical, functional, psychological, social, environmental - ending in **one problem list, one goal-based plan, one responsible clinician**. A process, not a questionnaire.`],[`Who needs it`,`**Target frailty, not age**: 60+ with a **geriatric giant** (fall, incontinence, immobility, confusion, weight loss), **5 or more drugs**, recent discharge, carer not coping; **CFS 5 or more** = full CGA, CFS 4 = targeted review.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Domains**: medical, functional (ADL/IADL), mobility, cognition, mood, nutrition, social-financial, environment, goals. **Geriatric giants** (Isaacs) + iatrogenesis; **5 Ms** (Mind, Mobility, Medications, Multicomplexity, Matters Most); **WHO ICOPE** 6 intrinsic-capacity domains.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cellular hallmarks (senescence, inflammageing) -> **homeostenosis** (loss of organ reserve) -> small stressor tips weakest system -> **atypical presentation** (fall, confusion) -> function is the most sensitive vital sign.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Function 6 months ago vs now** (ADL lost in weeks = disease); **IADL loss earliest sign of dementia**; STEADI 3 questions; **brown-bag drug review**; informant memory history; continence; weight, teeth; money control; **interview alone**; carer strain; **what matters most**.`],[`Key examination`,`**Watch rise and walk**; **TUG 12 s or more** = fall risk; gait speed under 0.8 m/s; **lying-standing BP** (fall 20/10); weight, calf circumference; **grip under 28/18 kg (AWGS 2025, 65+)**; vision, whispered voice, mouth, feet; Mini-Cog/HMSE, GDS-15; sacrum and heels.`],[`Screening tools and cut-offs`,`**Katz ADL** 0-6; **Lawton IADL** 0-8; **Barthel** under 60 dependent; **Mini-Cog 2 or less**, **MoCA under 26** (+1 if schooling 12 years or less); **GDS-15 5 or more**; **MNA-SF 0-7 malnourished, 8-11 at risk**; **CFS 5 or more frail**.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`CBC; creatinine with **Cockcroft-Gault CrCl** ((140 - age) x wt / (72 x Cr), x 0.85 women); Na, K; glucose/HbA1c, TSH, B12; 25-OH D if falls; **urine only with symptoms** (asymptomatic bacteriuria); ECG before donepezil. **No 'geriatric profile'.**`],[`Treatment - first line (plan)`,`One ranked problem list; **functional goal** per problem; written plan in local language; spread CGA over **2-3 visits**; **resistance + balance exercise 2-3 times/week**; protein **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day**; home safety; sensory and dental correction (Vayoshri).`],[`Drugs after CGA`,`**Start low, go slow, but go**; deprescribe (Beers 2023, STOPP/START v3); **cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly x 8 then 800-2000 IU daily**; **sertraline 25 mg** -> 50 mg (Na at 2-4 weeks); **donepezil 5 mg** -> 10 mg after 4-6 weeks (pulse, ECG); **paracetamol max 3 g/day if frail or under 50 kg**.`],[`Targets in frailty`,`**HbA1c under 8.0%** in complex health, **no reliance on HbA1c in very complex/poor health** - avoid hypoglycaemia (ADA 2026); systolic BP about **130-150 mmHg** if standing BP drops; avoid glibenclamide.`],[`Vaccines`,`**Influenza yearly**; **pneumococcal from 50** (PCV20/PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23 after 1 year); **recombinant zoster** 2 doses 2-6 months apart from 50; **Td every 10 years**; **RSV single dose 75+ (50-74 if at risk)**.`],[`Special groups`,`Unschooled patient - **HMSE or vernacular MoCA, never unadjusted MMSE**; dementia - informant and capacity; carer - assess burden; joint family - share plan with consent; post-discharge - reconcile drugs within 1-2 weeks.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`**Acute confusion = delirium**; ADL lost in a month; weight loss over 5% in 6 months; head injury on anticoagulant (same-day CT); cannot bear weight; long lie; carer collapse; suspected abuse - refer NPHCE clinic / memory clinic / same day as needed.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **4-12 weeks** (sooner if frailer); repeat weight, TUG/gait speed, ADL, GDS-15, drug list; **within 1-2 weeks of discharge**; yearly cognition; Na after SSRI/thiazide; pulse after donepezil.`],[`Prevention / national programme`,`**NPHCE** (geriatric OPD, 10-bed district wards, Ayushman Arogya Mandir screening); **PM-JAY Vay Vandana** Rs 5 lakh/year for all 70+ (Oct 2024); **Elderline 14567**; **Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana**; MWPSC Act 2007 tribunal.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AWGS 2025**: sarcopenia = low grip + low mass, from age 50 (grip under 34/20 kg at 50-64); **ACIP 2025** pneumococcal from 50, RSV 50-74 at risk; **ADA 2026** HbA1c under 8.0% in complex older adults.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Ward-based CGA -> more patients **alive and at home at 12 months** (Cochrane 2017). Serum creatinine lies in the old - use CrCl. Function is the vital sign. Interview the elder alone. Watch them walk before the stethoscope.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`CGA in the OPD - from trigger to plan`,caption:`Condensed exam version: screen, assess by domain, act, review.`,steps:[{label:`Trigger: frailty, giant, 5+ drugs, discharge`,detail:`CFS 5 or more = full CGA; CFS 4 = targeted review; age alone is not a trigger`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Acute change in function or confusion?`,detail:`Delirium or acute illness until proved otherwise - treat that first, same day`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Visit 1 - drugs, function, mobility`,detail:`Brown-bag review, Katz/Lawton, TUG 12 s or more, lying-standing BP, grip under 28/18 kg`},{label:`Visit 2 - cognition, mood, nutrition`,detail:`Mini-Cog/HMSE with informant, GDS-15 5 or more, MNA-SF 0-7 malnourished`},{label:`Visit 3 - social, home, carer, goals`,detail:`Who controls money, abuse (alone), carer burden, what matters most`},{label:`One problem list, one plan, one clinician`,detail:`Functional goals, deprescribing, exercise, protein 1.0-1.2 g/kg, vaccines, schemes`,tone:`good`},{label:`Review in 4-12 weeks`,detail:`Repeat weight, TUG, ADL, GDS-15, drug list; within 1-2 weeks of any discharge`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`CGA screening tools - cut-offs to write`,columns:[`Domain`,`Tool`,`Abnormal`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Basic ADL`,`Katz 0-6`,`4 moderate, 2 or less severe dependence`,`Ceiling effect`],[`IADL`,`Lawton 0-8`,`Any fall from baseline`,`Earliest dementia marker`],[`Cognition`,`Mini-Cog / MoCA / HMSE`,`Mini-Cog 2 or less; MoCA under 26`,`MMSE penalises the unschooled`],[`Mood`,`GDS-15`,`5 or more suggests depression`,`Better than PHQ-9 in the physically ill`],[`Nutrition`,`MNA-SF 0-14`,`0-7 malnourished, 8-11 at risk`,`Weight loss over 5% in 6 months matters regardless`],[`Mobility`,`TUG / gait speed`,`TUG 12 s or more; gait under 0.8 m/s`,`TUG over 20 s = dependence`],[`Strength`,`Grip dynamometer (AWGS 2025)`,`Under 28/18 kg at 65+; under 34/20 kg at 50-64`,`Needs low muscle mass too for sarcopenia`],[`Frailty`,`Clinical Frailty Scale 1-9`,`5 or more frail`,`Score status 2 weeks before illness`]]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-falls":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **fall** is coming to rest unintentionally on the ground or a lower level.`,`Falls due to stroke, seizure or overwhelming force are excluded.`,`About **1 in 3 over 65** and 1 in 2 over 80 fall each year.`,`About 10% of falls cause serious injury and **1-2% a hip fracture**.`,`A new fall in a frail person is a **symptom of acute illness** until proved otherwise.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**World Falls Guidelines 2022** sort people into low, intermediate and high risk.`,`**Low risk**: no fall, or one non-severe fall without gait problems.`,`**Intermediate risk**: a non-severe fall with gait or balance impairment.`,`**High risk**: injury, 2 or more falls, frailty, **long lie** or suspected syncope.`,`By mechanism: mechanical, syncopal, drop attack and unexplained.`,`Treat an **unexplained fall as possible syncope**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Balance needs three inputs: **vision, vestibular function and proprioception**.`,`Ageing slows central processing, so talking while walking disturbs gait.`,`**Sarcopenia** makes the quick stepping reflex too slow to save a trip.`,`A blunted baroreflex lets cerebral perfusion fall on standing: **orthostatic hypotension**.`,`Sedatives, alpha-blockers and anticholinergics add to every step of failure.`,`Older people fall **sideways onto the hip**, so osteoporotic bone breaks.`,`Fear of falling leads to inactivity, weakness and the next fall.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask all over 65 yearly the **3 STEADI questions**: fallen, unsteady, worried about falling.`,`Ask exactly what they were doing: rising, turning the head, after a meal, at night.`,`Ask whether **consciousness was lost**; if so the work-up is for syncope.`,`Ask how long they lay; a **lie over 1 hour** marks high risk.`,`Ask about head strike and whether they take **warfarin, a DOAC or antiplatelet**.`,`Take a full drug history, including sleeping tablets, cough syrups and recent dose changes.`,`Ask about nocturia, vision, footwear, memory, fear of falling and home hazards.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure **lying and standing BP** at 1 and 3 minutes.`,`A drop of **20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic** is orthostatic hypotension.`,`Check pulse rhythm and listen for the murmur of **aortic stenosis**.`,`**Timed Up and Go 12 seconds or more** predicts falls.`,`Do the 30-second chair stand and the 4-stage balance test.`,`Watch the gait; examine for neuropathy, parkinsonism and cerebellar signs.`,`Check vision, cataract, feet, footwear and the height of the walking aid.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`There is **no routine falls panel**; let the history guide tests.`,`**ECG** for syncope, unexplained falls or cardiac findings.`,`Haemoglobin, glucose, sodium, potassium, calcium and creatinine.`,`Echocardiography for an ejection systolic murmur or exertional syncope.`,`**CT head within 8 hours** after head injury on an anticoagulant, even if alert.`,`After a long lie, check **CK**, potassium and renal function for rhabdomyolysis.`,`Use **FRAX (India model)** and DXA where available to decide bone treatment.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Strength plus balance exercise**, 3 hours a week and sustained, is the best intervention.`,`**Walking alone does not reduce falls**.`,`The **Otago programme** cuts falls by about 35% in the over-80s.`,`Home fixes: grab rails, raised Western commode, non-slip floor, lit path to the toilet.`,`Advise fitted shoes, not loose chappals, and single-vision glasses outdoors.`,`Expedite first-eye **cataract surgery**.`,`Teach how to get up from the floor and how to call for help.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Deprescribing fall-risk drugs** is the highest-yield prescription.`,`Taper benzodiazepines by about **25% every 2 weeks**; never stop abruptly.`,`Reduce antihypertensives if standing systolic BP is under 110 mmHg.`,`Stop **glibenclamide**; relax HbA1c to under 8.0% in frailty.`,`Persistent orthostatic hypotension: **fludrocortisone** or **midodrine** after non-drug measures.`,`Protect bone with **alendronate**, zoledronic acid or denosumab when indicated.`,`**Do not stop anticoagulation in AF** because of falls.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fall with loss of consciousness: treat as **syncope**; same-day ECG.`,`Cannot bear weight or shortened, externally rotated leg: **hip fracture** until proved otherwise.`,`Hip fracture surgery should be on the day of or day after admission.`,`Headache or drowsiness days after a fall suggests **chronic subdural haematoma**.`,`Early backward falls with vertical gaze palsy suggest progressive supranuclear palsy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review **within 4-6 weeks** after a falls plan, then yearly.`,`Re-screen after every admission, drug change or new illness.`,`Refer to cardiology for recurrent unexplained falls with bradycardia.`,`Refer to physiotherapy, occupational therapy and the NPHCE geriatric clinic.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in falls`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Fludrocortisone`,`0.05-0.1 mg oral morning (max 0.3 mg)`,`Review at 2-4 weeks`,`Supine hypertension, hypokalaemia, oedema`],[`Midodrine`,`2.5 mg oral TDS, up to 10 mg TDS`,`Daytime doses only`,`Supine hypertension; not within 4 h of lying`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU oral weekly`,`8 weeks, then 800-1000 IU daily`,`Never give large annual boluses`],[`Calcium carbonate`,`500 mg elemental once or twice daily`,`With bone therapy if diet low`,`Constipation; target 1000-1200 mg total`],[`Alendronate`,`70 mg oral once weekly`,`5 years, then review`,`Upright 30 min; avoid if CrCl under 35`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`5 mg IV over 15+ minutes yearly`,`3 years, then review`,`Flu-like reaction; check calcium first`],[`Denosumab`,`60 mg SC every 6 months`,`Long term`,`Hypocalcaemia; rebound fractures if stopped`],[`Benzodiazepine taper`,`Cut about 25% every 2 weeks`,`Over 2-4 months`,`Rebound insomnia; seizures if abrupt`]]},{heading:`Falls risk groups (World Falls Guidelines 2022)`,columns:[`Risk group`,`Who`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Low`,`No fall, or one non-severe fall, normal gait`,`Education, general exercise, re-screen yearly`],[`Intermediate`,`Non-severe fall with gait or balance problem`,`Strength and balance programme`],[`High`,`Injury, 2+ falls, frailty, long lie, syncope`,`Full multifactorial assessment and intervention`]]},{heading:`Bedside tests and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`How`,`Abnormal`],rows:[[`Orthostatic BP`,`Lie 5 min, stand, measure at 1 and 3 min`,`Drop 20 systolic or 10 diastolic`],[`Timed Up and Go`,`Rise, walk 3 m, turn, return, sit`,`12 s or more; over 20 s dependent`],[`Gait speed`,`Usual pace over 4 m`,`0.8 m/s or less`],[`4-stage balance`,`Four stances, 10 s each`,`Cannot hold tandem 10 s`],[`Berg Balance Scale`,`14 tasks scored 0-4`,`Under 45 of 56`]]}],remember:[`STEADI: Fallen? Unsteady? Worried? One yes means assess.`,`Lying and standing BP is the most skipped test in a faller.`,`The best prescription after a fall is often a subtraction.`,`Walking alone does not prevent falls; strength plus balance does.`,`About 295 falls a year are needed before anticoagulation harms more than helps.`,`Never stop denosumab without a follow-on bisphosphonate.`,`Indian home: dry grab-railed bathroom, raised commode, lit path to the toilet.`]},updated:[`ADA 2026: in fallers with diabetes relax HbA1c to under 8.0% (complex health) - replaces 7.5-8.5%`,`AWGS 2025: gait speed is now an outcome measure, not a sarcopenia criterion; EWGSOP2 0.8 m/s or less still marks low performance`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Falls in the older person`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Inadvertently coming to rest on the ground or lower level**, excluding stroke, seizure or overwhelming force (WHO). A new fall in a frail person is a **symptom of acute illness** until proved otherwise.`],[`Burden`,`**1 in 3 over 65, 1 in 2 over 80** fall yearly; **10% serious injury, 1-2% hip fracture**; LASI about 13% of Indians 60+ in 2 years (under-reported).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**World Falls Guidelines 2022**: **low** (no fall / one non-severe, no gait problem), **intermediate** (non-severe fall + gait/balance impairment), **high** (injury, **2+ falls**, frailty, **long lie over 1 h**, suspected syncope). Mechanism: mechanical, syncopal, drop attack, unexplained (treat as syncope).`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`Vision (cataract, varifocals), vestibular (BPPV), neuropathy, **sarcopenia**, dementia, Parkinson disease, **orthostatic hypotension**, **FRIDs** (benzodiazepines, Z-drugs, antipsychotics, alpha-blockers, anticholinergics, diuretics), nocturia, foot problems, home hazards. Risk 8% with none -> **78% with 4+ factors**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Ageing degrades **vision + vestibular + proprioception** -> slow central processing, dual-task cost -> **type II fibre loss** slows stepping strategy -> **blunted baroreflex** -> trip or postural drop -> sideways fall on trochanter + osteoporosis -> hip fracture; long lie -> rhabdomyolysis.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**3 STEADI questions yearly** (fallen, unsteady, worried); what were you doing; **loss of consciousness** (syncope work-up); long lie; head strike on **anticoagulant**; full drug list incl. OTC and recent dose changes; nocturia; vision; home; fear of falling; FRAX risks.`],[`Key examination`,`**Lying-standing BP at 1 and 3 min** (fall 20/10 mmHg); pulse, **aortic stenosis murmur**; gait pattern; **TUG 12 s or more**; **30-s chair stand**, **4-stage balance** (tandem 10 s); Romberg, Dix-Hallpike, vibration sense; visual acuity; feet and footwear.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**ECG** in syncope/unexplained fall (Holter if arrhythmia suspected); Hb, glucose, Na, K, Ca, creatinine; TSH, B12, 25-OH D as indicated; **CK after long lie**; **CT head within 8 h** if on anticoagulant; hip X-ray then MRI if occult fracture; **FRAX/DXA**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Strength + challenging balance exercise 3 h/week, long term** (Otago - 35% fewer falls in over-80s); **deprescribe FRIDs**; OT home hazard modification; cataract surgery; single-vision distance glasses outdoors; proper footwear; teach getting up from floor.`],[`Drugs`,`**Benzodiazepine taper 10-25% every 2-4 weeks**; antipsychotic review 6-12 weeks; OH: **fludrocortisone 0.05-0.1 mg** (max 0.3), **midodrine 2.5 -> 10 mg TDS** (not within 4 h of lying); **vitamin D 60,000 IU weekly x 8 then 800-1000 IU daily if deficient - no annual megadose**.`],[`Bone protection`,`**Alendronate 70 mg weekly** (empty stomach, 200 mL water, upright 30 min; avoid CrCl under 35); **zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly**; **denosumab 60 mg SC 6-monthly - never stop without bisphosphonate**; calcium to 1000-1200 mg/day; review after 5 years oral / 3 years IV.`],[`Special groups`,`**AF on anticoagulant - do not stop** (about 295 falls/year to outweigh benefit); dementia - stop sedatives, supervision; **Parkinson disease - backward falls**, cueing; PSP if vertical gaze palsy; diabetes - **HbA1c under 8.0%**, stop glibenclamide.`],[`Complications`,`Hip, wrist, humerus, vertebral fractures; **subdural haematoma** (delayed); **rhabdomyolysis, AKI, hyperkalaemia**, hypothermia, pressure injury, aspiration after long lie; **post-fall syndrome** (fear -> inactivity -> more falls).`],[`Red flags and referral`,`LOC or unexplained fall with facial injury (syncope); head injury on anticoagulant; cannot bear weight; long lie; fall with fever/confusion; delayed headache or drowsiness; early backward falls with gaze palsy.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **4-6 weeks** after plan, then yearly STEADI; recheck lying-standing BP after each antihypertensive change, Na 2-4 weeks after SSRI/thiazide; TUG/chair stand for exercise benefit; rescreen after every admission.`],[`Prevention / national programme`,`**NPHCE** clinics; Indian home package - **Western commode, grab bars, night light to toilet, bed not floor mat**; walking aids free under **Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana**; **Elderline 14567**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**World Falls Guidelines 2022** risk stratification and **NICE NG249 (2025)** remain current; ADA 2026 relaxed HbA1c under 8.0%; AWGS 2025 keeps gait speed as an outcome.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Lying-standing BP is the most rewarding, most skipped test. Walking alone does not prevent falls. The best prescription is often a subtraction. Bed rails and restraints do not reduce falls.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Falls risk stratification - World Falls Guidelines 2022`,caption:`Opportunistic case finding to the right level of intervention.`,steps:[{label:`Ask: fallen, unsteady or worried about falling?`,detail:`Yearly in everyone 65+ (and 50-64 at higher risk)`},{label:`Injury, 2+ falls, frailty, long lie, syncope?`,detail:`Any one = HIGH risk`,tone:`decision`},{label:`High risk - multifactorial assessment`,detail:`Lying-standing BP, ECG, drugs, vision, gait, cognition, feet, home, FRAX`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Gait or balance impaired?`,detail:`TUG over 15 s or gait speed 0.8 m/s or less = INTERMEDIATE`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Intermediate - strength and balance programme`,detail:`Otago or group exercise 3 h/week plus education`},{label:`Low risk - education and general exercise`,detail:`Rescreen yearly`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Intervention ladder after a fall - strongest evidence first`,steps:[{label:`Treat injury and the precipitant`,detail:`CT head within 8 h on anticoagulant; hip surgery day of or after admission; CK after long lie`},{label:`Strength and balance exercise`,detail:`Progressive, 3 h/week, sustained; Otago at home for the over-80s`},{label:`Deprescribe fall-risk drugs`,detail:`Benzodiazepines (taper 10-25% every 2-4 weeks), antipsychotics, alpha-blockers, anticholinergics`},{label:`Fix BP, vision, feet and home`,detail:`OH: fludrocortisone 0.05-0.1 mg or midodrine 2.5-10 mg TDS; cataract surgery; grab bars, night light`},{label:`Bone protection`,detail:`Alendronate 70 mg weekly, zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly or denosumab 60 mg 6-monthly; vitamin D if deficient`,tone:`good`}]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-polypharmacy":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Polypharmacy** is 5 or more regular medicines; **hyperpolypharmacy** is 10 or more.`,`The count triggers a review; it is not a diagnosis.`,`**Inappropriate polypharmacy** means a drug whose harm outweighs its benefit for this patient now.`,`**Deprescribing** is planned, supervised dose reduction or stopping, aiming at better outcomes.`,`Adverse drug reactions cause **5-10% of admissions** in older people; most are preventable.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Explicit tools**: Beers 2023 (US) and **STOPP/START v3** (European, 133 STOPP, 57 START).`,`**Implicit tools**: the Medication Appropriateness Index judges each drug on its merits.`,`**Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden** total of 3 or more predicts delirium, falls and death.`,`ADR types: **A augmented** (dose-related, commonest in elderly), B bizarre, C chronic, D delayed.`,`E is end-of-use (withdrawal) and F is failure of therapy.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Less body water gives **higher peaks of water-soluble drugs** such as digoxin and lithium.`,`More body fat makes **fat-soluble drugs** such as diazepam last for days.`,`Liver blood flow falls 30-40%, so first-pass and CYP oxidation decline.`,`GFR falls but **creatinine stays normal**, so dose by Cockcroft-Gault clearance.`,`The older brain is more sensitive to sedatives, opioids and anticholinergics.`,`A **prescribing cascade** treats a side effect as a new disease, such as amlodipine oedema with furosemide.`,`**Triple whammy**: NSAID plus ACE inhibitor or ARB plus diuretic causes acute kidney injury.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Do a **brown-bag review** of every strip, drop, tonic, churna and homeopathic remedy.`,`For each drug ask who prescribed it, for what, and whether it still helps.`,`Ask about over-the-counter sleeping tablets, cough syrups, painkillers and borrowed drugs.`,`Treat **every new symptom as a possible adverse drug reaction**.`,`Ask about missed doses, monthly cost and recent admissions or new specialists.`,`Ask what matters most and the patient's sense of remaining life.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure **lying and standing BP** before changing antihypertensives.`,`Bradycardia suggests beta-blocker, digoxin, donepezil or verapamil excess.`,`Screen for delirium with the **4AT**; new inattention after a drug change is drug-induced.`,`Look for anticholinergic signs: dry mouth, palpable bladder, faecal loading.`,`Look for extrapyramidal signs from metoclopramide, prochlorperazine or flunarizine.`,`Check the patient can read the label, open the strip and swallow the tablet.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Creatinine with Cockcroft-Gault clearance** for dosing DOACs, metformin, gabapentinoids and digoxin.`,`Sodium and potassium on diuretics, SSRIs, ACE inhibitors or spironolactone.`,`Drug levels only for narrow-index drugs: **digoxin 0.5-0.9 ng/mL**, lithium, phenytoin, INR.`,`ECG with QTc when combining QT-prolonging drugs; stop one if **QTc over 500 ms**.`,`Vitamin B12 on long-term metformin or PPI.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Step 1: **list everything**, including OTC, AYUSH and alcohol.`,`Step 2: weigh each drug's **time to benefit against remaining life expectancy**.`,`Step 3: screen with Beers and STOPP/START, and look for cascades and duplicates.`,`Step 4: stop the highest-harm, lowest-benefit drug first, **one at a time**.`,`Step 5: **taper drugs with withdrawal syndromes**; stop others outright.`,`Step 6: agree, document and review in **1-4 weeks**.`,`Frame it as a **reversible trial**; the drug can be restarted.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Beers 2023: **no aspirin for primary prevention**; prefer apixaban over rivaroxaban and warfarin.`,`Beers 2023: avoid sulfonylureas as first or second line; never glibenclamide.`,`Avoid PPIs beyond 8 weeks without a clear indication; step down gradually.`,`Swap chlorpheniramine for **cetirizine**, oxybutynin for bladder training or mirabegron.`,`Replace benzodiazepines with CBT-I, or **melatonin prolonged-release 2 mg** if needed.`,`**START** omissions matter too: statin, anticoagulant in AF, bisphosphonate, laxative with opioids.`,`Never combine **oxybutynin with donepezil**; they are pharmacological opposites.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`New confusion or a fall soon after a drug change: assume the drug.`,`**Sodium under 125 mmol/L** on thiazide or SSRI needs urgent action.`,`Sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia recurs for hours, so **admit**.`,`Yellow vision, nausea or bradycardia on digoxin: same-day level and potassium.`,`Abrupt levodopa withdrawal can cause a neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome.`,`Cushingoid features after tonics or churnas suggest **hidden steroids**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review **1-4 weeks after each drug** is stopped or tapered.`,`Do a structured medication review **at least yearly** in anyone on 5 or more drugs.`,`Report ADRs to **PvPI** on the helpline 1800-180-3024 or the app.`,`Prescribe from **NLEM 2022** and use Janaushadhi Kendras to cut cost.`,`Document every stopped drug with reason, taper and review date.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Deprescribing tapers and safer substitutes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Benzodiazepine taper`,`Cut 10-25% of dose every 2-4 weeks`,`2-6 months in total`,`Rebound insomnia 1-2 weeks; seizures if abrupt`],[`Melatonin prolonged-release`,`2 mg oral 1-2 h before bed`,`Up to 13 weeks`,`Mild headache, daytime drowsiness`],[`PPI step-down (pantoprazole)`,`20 mg daily, then alternate days`,`2-4 weeks per step, then on demand`,`Rebound dyspepsia; famotidine 20 mg at night`],[`Cetirizine`,`5-10 mg oral daily (5 mg if CrCl under 50)`,`As needed`,`Mild drowsiness`],[`Mirabegron`,`25 mg oral daily, max 50 mg`,`Long term`,`Raised BP; avoid if BP 180/110 or higher`],[`Linagliptin`,`5 mg oral daily`,`Long term`,`No renal adjustment; rare pancreatitis`],[`Apixaban`,`5 mg BD; 2.5 mg BD if 2 of 3 criteria`,`Long term`,`Criteria: 80+, 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+`],[`Pregabalin`,`25-75 mg at night; cap by clearance`,`Taper over at least 1 week`,`Dizziness, falls; max 300 mg if CrCl 30-60`],[`Digoxin`,`62.5-125 microgram oral daily`,`Long term with levels`,`Toxicity worse with low potassium`]]},{heading:`Beers 2023: avoid and use instead`,columns:[`Drug or class`,`Why avoid`,`Use instead`],rows:[[`Chlorpheniramine, hydroxyzine, promethazine`,`Anticholinergic: confusion, retention, falls`,`Cetirizine or fexofenadine`],[`Benzodiazepines and Z-drugs`,`Delirium, falls, fracture`,`CBT-I, sleep hygiene, melatonin`],[`Antipsychotics for dementia behaviour`,`Stroke, death, parkinsonism`,`Non-drug care; lowest dose if unavoidable`],[`Amitriptyline`,`Anticholinergic, postural drop`,`Duloxetine or dosed gabapentinoid`],[`Glibenclamide`,`Prolonged hypoglycaemia`,`Metformin, DPP-4 inhibitor, gliclazide`],[`Aspirin for primary prevention`,`Bleeding outweighs benefit`,`Stop it`],[`PPI beyond 8 weeks`,`C. difficile, low B12 and magnesium, fracture`,`Step down to on demand`],[`Long-term oral NSAIDs`,`GI bleed, AKI, heart failure`,`Paracetamol, topical NSAID`]]},{heading:`STOPP and START v3 - examples`,columns:[`Type`,`Criterion`,`Reason`],rows:[[`STOPP`,`Drug without indication or beyond duration`,`Risk without purpose`],[`STOPP`,`Benzodiazepine for 4 weeks or more`,`Benefit fades, harm stays`],[`STOPP`,`NSAID with heart failure or CrCl under 50`,`Sodium retention and AKI`],[`STOPP`,`Antimuscarinic with dementia, glaucoma or prostatism`,`Worsens each condition`],[`START`,`Statin in atherosclerotic disease`,`Secondary prevention works in old age`],[`START`,`Anticoagulant in AF`,`Prevents stroke`],[`START`,`Laxative with regular opioid`,`No tolerance to constipation`]]}],remember:[`Any new symptom in an older person is an adverse drug effect until proved otherwise.`,`Time to benefit longer than life expectancy: stop the drug.`,`One drug at a time, taper the risky ones, set a review date.`,`Recited list never equals the brown bag.`,`START omissions cost as many marks as STOPP excesses.`,`Donepezil plus oxybutynin on one list is a prescribing cascade.`,`Triple whammy: NSAID, ACE inhibitor or ARB, diuretic.`]},updated:[`ADA 2026: relax HbA1c in frail older adults to under 8.0% (avoid reliance on HbA1c in very complex health) - replaces 7.5-8.5%`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Polypharmacy and deprescribing`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Polypharmacy 5 or more** regular drugs, **hyperpolypharmacy 10 or more**; the problem is **inappropriate polypharmacy** - no indication, no benefit in remaining life, harm outweighs benefit. **Deprescribing** = planned, supervised dose reduction or stopping.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Explicit tools**: **AGS Beers 2023** (5 tables) and **STOPP/START v3 2023** (133 STOPP, 57 START). **Implicit**: Medication Appropriateness Index, **ACB scale (total 3 or more = harm)**. ADR types A-F (Rawlins-Thompson) - most in elderly are type A.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`Multimorbidity, many prescribers, no shared record, **OTC Schedule H sales**, AYUSH tonics with hidden steroids/NSAIDs, transitions of care, **prescribing cascades**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Less body water -> **higher peaks of digoxin, lithium**; more fat -> **diazepam accumulates**; low albumin -> free phenytoin/warfarin up; liver flow down 30-40% -> less first-pass; **GFR falls but creatinine stays normal**; sensitive brain -> delirium, falls.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Brown-bag review** (strips, drops, OTC, churnas); for each drug - who, when, why, still working, how taken; **every new symptom = ADR** until proved otherwise; adherence and cost; recent discharge; **time to benefit vs life expectancy**; goals.`],[`Key examination`,`**Lying-standing BP**; pulse (bradycardia - beta-blocker, digoxin, donepezil); **4AT**; anticholinergic signs (palpable bladder, faecal loading); extrapyramidal signs; oedema (CCB); bruising; weight; can they open and read the strip.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Cockcroft-Gault CrCl** for DOACs, metformin, gabapentinoids, digoxin; Na, K; **digoxin 0.5-0.9 ng/mL**; INR; **QTc** (over 500 ms or rise over 60 ms = change drug); B12 on metformin/PPI; Mg on PPI + diuretic.`],[`The deprescribing process`,`**1 list everything -> 2 indication, benefit, time to benefit -> 3 identify harm (Beers/STOPP, ACB, cascades, renal) -> 4 prioritise, one drug at a time -> 5 taper drugs with withdrawal -> 6 agree, document, review 1-4 weeks.** Frame as a reversible trial.`],[`Beers 2023 key avoid list`,`First-generation antihistamines, **benzodiazepines/Z-drugs**, tricyclics, muscle relaxants, **3+ CNS-active drugs**, **aspirin for primary prevention**, **warfarin first-line (prefer apixaban over rivaroxaban)**, **sulfonylureas first/second-line (never glibenclamide)**, PPI beyond 8 weeks, digoxin over 0.125 mg, alpha-blockers for BP.`],[`START omissions`,`**Statin** in atherosclerotic disease, **ACEI/ARB + beta-blocker in HFrEF**, **anticoagulant in AF**, **bisphosphonate + vitamin D + calcium** in osteoporosis/steroids, **laxative with regular opioid**, influenza and pneumococcal vaccines.`],[`Tapers and swaps (doses)`,`**Benzodiazepine 10-25% every 2-4 weeks** (alprazolam 0.5 = diazepam 10 mg); **PPI halve -> alternate days -> on demand**; chlorpheniramine -> **cetirizine 5-10 mg**; oxybutynin -> **mirabegron 25 mg**; glibenclamide -> **linagliptin 5 mg**; **apixaban 2.5 mg BD only if 2 of age 80+, wt 60 kg or less, Cr 1.5 or more**; pregabalin max 300/150/75 mg/day at CrCl 30-60/15-30/under 15.`],[`Special groups`,`**CKD** - dose by CrCl, metformin halve at eGFR 30-44, stop under 30; **dementia** - no anticholinergics, never oxybutynin + donepezil; **frailty** - **HbA1c under 8.0%** (ADA 2026), SBP 130-150 if postural drop; end of life - stop preventive drugs.`],[`Complications`,`Falls, delirium, **triple whammy AKI** (NSAID + ACEI/ARB + diuretic), hyperkalaemia, **hyponatraemia** (SSRI, thiazide), GI bleed, serotonin syndrome, QT prolongation, sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia, withdrawal syndromes.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`New confusion or fall after a drug change; **Na under 125**; **K over 6.0**; sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia (admit - recurs); bleeding on anticoagulant; digoxin or lithium toxicity; NMS-like syndrome after levodopa stopped.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **1-4 weeks after each stop**; **structured medication review yearly** in 5+ drugs and after any admission, fall or new specialist; Na at 2-4 weeks (SSRI/thiazide); K and creatinine 1-2 weeks after ACEI/ARB/spironolactone.`],[`Prevention / national programme`,`**NLEM 2022** price-capped drugs, **Janaushadhi Kendras**, one pharmacy and one list; report ADRs to **PvPI (1800-180-3024)**; medicine reconciliation at every transition.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Beers 2023 and STOPP/START v3 remain the current editions (Beers update expected); **ADA 2026** relaxed HbA1c to under 8.0% in complex older adults.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Any new symptom is an ADR until proved otherwise. Stop one drug at a time. START omissions cost as many marks as STOPP excesses. Oxybutynin with donepezil is a cascade.`]]},flows:[{kind:`cycle`,heading:`The deprescribing cycle - write it as six steps`,steps:[{label:`List every drug (brown bag)`,detail:`Include OTC, AYUSH, eye drops, alcohol`},{label:`Indication, benefit, time to benefit`,detail:`Against remaining life expectancy and goals`},{label:`Identify harm`,detail:`Beers 2023, STOPP/START v3, ACB 3 or more, cascades, renal dose`},{label:`Prioritise - one drug at a time`,detail:`Highest harm, lowest benefit first`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Taper or stop`,detail:`Taper benzodiazepines, beta-blockers, SSRIs, PPIs, steroids, gabapentinoids`},{label:`Agree, document, review in 1-4 weeks`,detail:`Reversible trial; restart only if indication returns`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`High-yield swaps - stop this, use that`,columns:[`Stop`,`Why`,`Safer choice`],rows:[[`Chlorpheniramine, promethazine`,`Anticholinergic - delirium, retention`,`Cetirizine 5-10 mg or fexofenadine 120-180 mg`],[`Alprazolam / zolpidem`,`Falls, delirium, fracture`,`CBT-I; melatonin PR 2 mg up to 13 weeks`],[`Oxybutynin`,`Anticholinergic; cascade with donepezil`,`Bladder training, then mirabegron 25 mg`],[`Glibenclamide`,`Prolonged hypoglycaemia`,`Linagliptin 5 mg or gliclazide MR 30 mg`],[`Amitriptyline for pain`,`Anticholinergic, postural drop`,`Duloxetine 30 mg or renally dosed gabapentinoid`],[`Aspirin primary prevention`,`Bleeding exceeds benefit`,`Stop; keep only for secondary prevention`],[`Long-term PPI without indication`,`C. difficile, low Mg and B12, fracture`,`Step down to on-demand; famotidine 20 mg`]]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-palliative-symptom-control":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Palliative care** is active holistic care of people with serious health-related suffering (IAHPC 2018).`,`It is based on **need, not prognosis**, and starts early alongside disease treatment.`,`It **neither hastens nor postpones death**; this separates it from euthanasia.`,`**Total pain** (Cicely Saunders) has physical, psychological, social and spiritual parts.`,`About 7-10 million Indians need palliative care yearly; under 4% receive it.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Pain by mechanism: **nociceptive somatic**, nociceptive visceral, **neuropathic** and mixed.`,`Breakthrough pain is **incident**, **end-of-dose** or spontaneous.`,`**WHO ladder**: non-opioid for mild, weak opioid for moderate, strong opioid for severe pain.`,`Adjuvants can be added at any step; start at step 3 if pain is severe.`,`WHO rules: **by mouth, by the clock, by the ladder**, for the individual.`,`**ECOG 3-4** or PPS 30-50% usually means best supportive care.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Tissue injury activates A-delta and C fibres, which synapse in the dorsal horn.`,`Repeated firing opens **NMDA receptors**, causing wind-up and central sensitisation.`,`Morphine acts on **mu receptors**, cutting transmitter release in the dorsal horn.`,`Morphine metabolites are renally cleared; in renal failure they cause **myoclonus and drowsiness**.`,`Gut mu receptors slow peristalsis, and **no tolerance develops to constipation**.`,`**Sedation always precedes respiratory depression**; pain itself antagonises it.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Screen all symptoms with **ESAS-r**; 7-10 is severe and needs urgent control.`,`Take a separate history for each pain; the **mechanism chooses the drug**.`,`Count **rescue doses used in 24 hours** and check for end-of-dose pain.`,`Ask about bowels, nausea, drowsiness, **jerks** and hallucinations on opioids.`,`Ask about back pain with leg weakness, thirst and confusion, and facial swelling.`,`Ask the family's beliefs about morphine; **opiophobia** stops doses being given.`,`Ask preferred place of care and death, and who should decide.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine each pain site for bone tenderness, hepatomegaly and allodynia.`,`Do a **rectal examination** before escalating laxatives; impaction causes overflow.`,`Opioid toxicity: **pinpoint pupils, myoclonus**, respiratory rate under 8.`,`Examine the legs neurologically in back pain for **spinal cord compression**.`,`Check mouth for thrush, pressure areas, and bladder for retention.`,`Record performance status (ECOG or PPS) each visit.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Test only when the result will change care that fits the goals.`,`**Creatinine and eGFR** before choosing an opioid.`,`eGFR under 30: avoid regular morphine and codeine; use **fentanyl or buprenorphine**.`,`**Corrected calcium** in confusion, thirst, polyuria or constipation.`,`Suspected cord compression: **whole-spine MRI within 24 hours**.`,`Stop routine bloods and observations in the last days.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Step 1: **paracetamol 1 g every 6 hours**, max 4 g (3 g if under 50 kg or frail).`,`Opioid-naive: **oral IR morphine 5-10 mg every 4 hours** by the clock.`,`Frail or eGFR 30-60: start **2.5-5 mg** every 4-6 hours.`,`**Breakthrough dose is one-sixth of the 24-hour dose**, repeatable hourly.`,`Every 24-48 hours, add rescue doses into the regular dose; step up no more than 30-50%.`,`Once stable, give modified-release: **24-hour total divided by 2**, twice daily.`,`Prescribe a **stimulant laxative with every opioid**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Oral to subcutaneous morphine is **2 to 1**: halve the 24-hour dose.`,`When switching opioids, **reduce the calculated dose by 25-50%**.`,`**Fentanyl patches** are for stable pain only, never for the opioid-naive.`,`Neuropathic pain: add **gabapentin, pregabalin**, amitriptyline or duloxetine.`,`**Dexamethasone 8 mg** for nerve compression or liver capsule pain; give before noon.`,`Breathlessness: **fan to the face** and low-dose morphine; oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%.`,`Choose antiemetics by cause: **haloperidol** for chemical, **metoclopramide** for stasis, **cyclizine** for vestibular.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Cord compression**: dexamethasone 16 mg now, MRI within 24 hours, same-day oncology.`,`**SVC obstruction**: sit up, dexamethasone 16 mg, urgent stenting or radiotherapy.`,`**Hypercalcaemia**: IV saline then zoledronic acid 4 mg IV, if fits the goals.`,`Opioid toxicity: dilute naloxone and give **20 microgram steps** titrated to breathing.`,`Catastrophic bleed: dark towels and **midazolam 10 mg** SC, IM or buccal.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NDPS Amendment Act 2014** created 'essential narcotic drugs' under central control.`,`A **Recognised Medical Institution** needs one approval to stock and dispense morphine.`,`Write total quantity in **words and figures**, with registration number.`,`Keep a stock register and take back unused opioids after death.`,`**National Programme for Palliative Care 2012**; Kerala's neighbourhood network is the model.`,`Without a syringe driver, a **24G butterfly** under the skin allows 4-hourly SC doses at home.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Key palliative drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Morphine IR oral`,`5-10 mg every 4 hours (2.5-5 mg if frail)`,`Titrate every 24-48 h; no ceiling`,`Constipation, nausea, drowsiness; myoclonus in toxicity`],[`Morphine MR oral`,`24-hour total divided by 2, 12-hourly`,`Long term with IR rescue`,`Never crush or split`],[`Fentanyl patch`,`12-100 microgram per hour`,`Change every 72 hours`,`Not for opioid-naive; heat increases absorption`],[`Senna or bisacodyl`,`Senna 15 mg or bisacodyl 5-10 mg at night`,`From the first opioid dose`,`Colic; avoid isphagula`],[`Haloperidol`,`0.5-1.5 mg oral or SC at night`,`While nausea persists`,`Extrapyramidal effects; avoid in Parkinson disease`],[`Metoclopramide`,`10 mg TDS before meals`,`While stasis persists`,`Not in complete obstruction`],[`Cyclizine`,`50 mg up to TDS; 150 mg per 24 h CSCI`,`As needed`,`Sedation; dilute in water, not saline`],[`Dexamethasone`,`8 mg daily for pain; 16 mg in cord compression`,`Taper to lowest dose`,`Hyperglycaemia, thrush; give before noon`],[`Midazolam`,`2.5-5 mg SC hourly; 10-30 mg per 24 h CSCI`,`Terminal agitation`,`Sedation, respiratory depression`],[`Hyoscine butylbromide`,`20 mg SC; 60-120 mg per 24 h CSCI`,`Start early for death rattle`,`Dry mouth, retention; little sedation`]]},{heading:`Opioid conversions to learn exactly`,columns:[`From`,`To`,`Ratio`,`Caution`],rows:[[`Oral morphine`,`SC morphine`,`Divide by 2`,`SC rescue is one-sixth of SC daily dose`],[`Oral morphine`,`IV morphine`,`Divide by 2 to 3`,`Use 3 if unsure`],[`Oral codeine`,`Oral morphine`,`Divide by 10`,`Prodrug; max 240 mg per day`],[`Oral tramadol`,`Oral morphine`,`Divide by 10`,`Max 400 mg per day; seizures`],[`Oral morphine`,`Oral oxycodone`,`Divide by 1.5`,`Useful in mild renal impairment`],[`Oral morphine 60-90 mg daily`,`Fentanyl patch`,`25 microgram per hour`,`Stable pain only`],[`24-hour regular dose`,`Breakthrough dose`,`One-sixth`,`Repeat hourly as needed`],[`Any opioid`,`Another opioid`,`Reduce by 25-50%`,`Incomplete cross-tolerance`]]},{heading:`Antiemetic by cause`,columns:[`Cause`,`Receptor`,`Drug`],rows:[[`Opioids, uraemia, hypercalcaemia`,`D2 at chemoreceptor trigger zone`,`Haloperidol`],[`Gastric stasis, squashed stomach`,`D2 plus 5-HT4 prokinetic`,`Metoclopramide or domperidone`],[`Vestibular, raised ICP, obstruction`,`H1 and muscarinic`,`Cyclizine`],[`Chemotherapy or radiotherapy`,`5-HT3`,`Ondansetron 4-8 mg BD`],[`Refractory or mixed`,`Broad spectrum`,`Levomepromazine 6.25 mg at night`],[`Malignant bowel obstruction`,`Antisecretory`,`Octreotide plus hyoscine butylbromide`]]}],remember:[`Breakthrough is one-sixth of the 24-hour dose.`,`Oral to SC morphine: halve it. Switching opioids: cut 25-50%.`,`Tolerance develops to nausea and sedation, never to constipation.`,`Sedation comes before respiratory depression.`,`Never mix metoclopramide with cyclizine; antimuscarinics block the prokinetic.`,`A fan to the face plus low-dose morphine beats oxygen when not hypoxic.`,`Since NDPS 2014, the barrier to morphine is opiophobia, not law.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Palliative symptom control and morphine`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Active holistic care of people with serious health-related suffering** from severe illness and their families (IAHPC 2018) - based on **need, not prognosis**; neither hastens nor postpones death; starts early alongside disease treatment.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Pain: **nociceptive somatic, visceral, neuropathic, mixed**; breakthrough: incident, end-of-dose, spontaneous; **WHO ladder** (Step 1 NRS 1-3, Step 2 4-6, Step 3 7-10, adjuvants any step); **total pain** (physical, psychological, social, spiritual); ECOG / PPS.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Tissue injury -> A-delta/C fibres -> dorsal horn -> **NMDA wind-up** (central sensitisation); morphine -> **mu receptor** -> less transmitter release; **M6G/M3G accumulate in renal failure** -> myoclonus, drowsiness; myenteric mu -> constipation (**no tolerance**).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**ESAS-r** (4-6 moderate, 7-10 severe); history of **each pain** - mechanism chooses drug; rescue doses used per 24 h; opioid side effects; **red flags** (cord compression, hypercalcaemia, SVC); opiophobia; preferred place of care; PAINAD in dementia.`],[`Key examination`,`Each pain site; **rectal exam before escalating laxatives**; opioid toxicity signs (pinpoint pupils, **myoclonus**, RR under 8); leg neurology in back pain; mouth (thrush); pressure areas; palpable bladder in restlessness; PPS.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Only if result changes care. **Creatinine/eGFR** before opioid choice (under 30 - avoid morphine, codeine); **corrected calcium** (Ca + 0.8 x (4 - albumin)); electrolytes, glucose; **whole-spine MRI within 24 h** for suspected MSCC.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Paracetamol 1 g 6-hourly** (3 g if frail); NSAID with PPI if no renal risk; **oral IR morphine 5-10 mg every 4 h** (2.5-5 mg if frail/renal), **double dose at bedtime**; **rescue = one-sixth of 24 h dose, hourly**; **senna 15 mg at night from first dose**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Every 24-48 h add regular + rescue doses and redistribute; **max 30-50% rise per step**; convert to **MR = 24 h total / 2, 12-hourly**; **no ceiling dose**; switching opioids - **reduce 25-50%**; eGFR under 30 - fentanyl or buprenorphine patch.`],[`Adjuvants`,`Neuropathic - **gabapentin 300-600 mg TDS**, pregabalin, amitriptyline 10-25 mg, duloxetine; **dexamethasone 8 mg** (nerve/capsule), **16 mg** (cord compression) before noon with PPI; bone - **zoledronic acid 4 mg** or denosumab 120 mg, **single 8 Gy radiotherapy**.`],[`Antiemetics by cause`,`Chemical (opioid, uraemia, Ca) - **haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg**; gastric stasis - **metoclopramide 10 mg TDS**; vestibular/raised ICP/obstruction - **cyclizine 50 mg TDS**; chemo - ondansetron; refractory - **levomepromazine 6.25 mg**; MBO - **octreotide 300-600 microgram + hyoscine butylbromide 60-120 mg/24 h**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Oral to SC morphine 2:1**; syringe driver 24 h mix of 2-3 drugs in **water for injection** (cyclizine precipitates in saline); 24-25G butterfly in chest/arm/abdomen; check site 4-hourly, re-site 3-4 days; **SC rescue = one-sixth of 24 h SC dose**; no driver - 4-hourly SC boluses through butterfly.`],[`Last days`,`Breathlessness - fan, **morphine 2.5-5 mg PO / 1-2 mg SC**, lorazepam 0.5-1 mg SL; delirium - **haloperidol 0.5-1 mg**, terminal agitation **midazolam 10-30 mg/24 h**; death rattle - **hyoscine butylbromide 20 mg SC** or glycopyrronium 200 microgram early; mouth care; stop routine bloods.`],[`Fentanyl and buprenorphine patches`,`**Fentanyl 12 microgram/h = 30-45 mg oral morphine/day**, 25 = 60-90 mg; change **72-hourly**, onset 12-24 h, stable pain only, **never opioid-naive**, heat increases absorption; **buprenorphine 5 microgram/h = about 12 mg morphine**, 7-day patch, safe in renal failure.`],[`Complications and emergencies`,`**MSCC** - dexamethasone 16 mg now, MRI 24 h; **SVC obstruction** - dexamethasone, stent; **hypercalcaemia** - saline 2-4 L + zoledronic acid 4 mg; **catastrophic bleed** - dark towels, midazolam 10 mg; **opioid toxicity** - naloxone 20 microgram IV steps.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`RR under 8 with unrousable sedation; new myoclonus (renal decline); back pain + leg weakness; confusion + polyuria; facial swelling; absolute constipation with vomiting; pain escalating despite titration - specialist palliative team.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **24-72 h** after starting/changing morphine, then weekly while titrating; ESAS target **3 or less**; recheck creatinine if myoclonus or drowsiness; taper dexamethasone weekly; bereavement contact; **take back unused opioids**.`],[`Prevention / national programme`,`**NPPC 2012** under NHM; Kerala Neighbourhood Network model; **NDPS Amendment Act 2014** - essential narcotic drugs, **one RMI approval** from State Drugs Controller (Rules 2015); oral morphine on **NLEM 2022**; palliative care in Ayushman Arogya Mandir package.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - WHO 2018 cancer pain guideline, PCF and NICE NG234 (MSCC, 2023) remain current; Indian access rules unchanged since the 2014-2015 NDPS amendments.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Rescue = one-sixth of 24 h dose. Oral:SC morphine 2:1. Never an opioid without a stimulant laxative. Sedation precedes respiratory depression. Fan plus low-dose morphine beats oxygen for the non-hypoxic.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Starting and titrating oral morphine - exam version`,steps:[{label:`Assess each pain and its mechanism`,detail:`Neuropathic, bone and visceral pain need adjuvants too`,tone:`decision`},{label:`eGFR under 30?`,detail:`Yes - avoid regular morphine/codeine; fentanyl or buprenorphine patch`,tone:`warn`},{label:`IR morphine 5-10 mg every 4 h by the clock`,detail:`2.5-5 mg if frail or eGFR 30-60; double dose at bedtime`},{label:`Rescue = one-sixth of 24 h dose, hourly`,detail:`Senna 15 mg at night from day 1; antiemetic for 3-5 days if nauseated`},{label:`Retitrate every 24-48 h`,detail:`Regular + rescue doses used = new 24 h total; rise no more than 30-50%`},{label:`Stable - convert to MR 12-hourly`,detail:`24 h total / 2; keep IR rescue; swallow whole`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Opioid conversions and antiemetic choice`,columns:[`Item`,`Rule`,`Caution`],rows:[[`Oral to SC morphine`,`Divide by 2`,`SC rescue one-sixth of 24 h SC dose`],[`Oral to IV morphine`,`Divide by 2-3 (3 if unsure)`,`Monitor sedation and RR`],[`Codeine / tramadol to morphine`,`Divide by 10`,`Tramadol max 400 mg (300 over 75)`],[`Morphine to oxycodone`,`Divide by 1.5`,`Mild-moderate renal impairment`],[`Morphine 60-90 mg/day`,`Fentanyl 25 microgram/h, 72-hourly`,`Not for opioid-naive or titration`],[`Switching opioid`,`Reduce calculated dose 25-50%`,`Incomplete cross-tolerance`],[`Chemical nausea`,`Haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg`,`Avoid in Parkinson, Lewy body`],[`Gastric stasis`,`Metoclopramide 10 mg TDS`,`Not in complete obstruction; not with cyclizine`],[`Vestibular, ICP, obstruction`,`Cyclizine 50 mg TDS (150 mg/24 h)`,`Dilute in water, not saline`]]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-consent-capacity-records":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Consent** is agreement to the same thing in the same sense (consensus ad idem).`,`Valid consent is from a **competent person with capacity**, voluntary, informed, specific, and given beforehand.`,`Consent can be **withdrawn at any time**.`,`**Capacity** is the ability to make a particular decision at a particular time.`,`**Competence** is legal status, such as age 18 and sound mind.`,`Confidentiality is a professional duty (Regulation 7.14) and a right under **Article 21 (Puttaswamy 2017)**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Implied consent**: routine non-invasive examination, such as holding out an arm for BP.`,`**Expressed oral**: minor procedures, recorded in the notes.`,`**Expressed written**: surgery, anaesthesia, transfusion, invasive tests, research and medico-legal cases.`,`**Proxy consent**: guardian or nominated representative when the patient lacks capacity.`,`**18 years** for consent to treatment; 12 or over may consent to examination only.`,`**Blanket consent** at admission is not valid for a specific procedure.`]},{title:`The law of consent`,points:[`**IPC 87-93 are now BNS 25-31**, in force from 1 July 2024.`,`**BNS 26 (IPC 88)** protects good-faith treatment with consent, even if harm results.`,`**BNS 28 (IPC 90)**: consent under fear, misconception, intoxication or by a child under 12 is void.`,`**BNS 30 (IPC 92)**: emergency treatment without consent is lawful by necessity.`,`**Samira Kohli 2008**: consent for diagnostic laparoscopy does not cover hysterectomy.`,`Samira Kohli also held that **a relative cannot consent for a competent adult**.`,`**Parmanand Katara 1989**: give emergency care first, without waiting for police.`]},{title:`Capacity - the test`,points:[`Capacity is **presumed, decision-specific and time-specific**.`,`**An unwise decision is not incapacity**.`,`**MHCA 2017 Section 4**: the person can understand, appreciate consequences and communicate.`,`The UK test adds the ability to **retain and weigh** the information.`,`First correct delirium, pain, hypoxia and sedation, and use an interpreter or hearing aid.`,`**The MMSE is not a capacity assessment**.`,`If capacity is absent, act in **best interests** with the least restrictive option.`]},{title:`Confidentiality and its exceptions`,points:[`Exception 1: the **patient's own consent**; relatives have no automatic right.`,`Exception 2: a **court order**, after pointing out the information is confidential.`,`Exception 3: **statute**, such as notifiable diseases and POCSO reporting.`,`Exception 4: **serious risk to an identified person** (Mr X v Hospital Z 1998).`,`**HIV Act 2017**: no disclosure of HIV status without consent or court order.`,`**DPDP Act 2023**: a clinic holding digital data is a data fiduciary with breach-notification duties.`,`Never post a clinical photograph without **separate written consent**.`]},{title:`Medical records`,points:[`Keep indoor records for **at least 3 years** (Regulation 1.3.1).`,`Give copies to the patient **within 72 hours** of a request.`,`Keep adult records 5-8 years in practice; medico-legal records 10 years or permanently.`,`A good entry is legible, **dated, timed and signed** with registration number.`,`Correct an error with a **single line**, dated and initialled; never erase or overwrite.`,`**Never alter a record after a complaint**; add only a labelled, dated addendum.`,`EMRs are admissible as evidence with a certificate under **BSA 63** (old IEA 65B).`]},{title:`Taking consent - step by step`,points:[`The doctor who will perform the procedure takes consent; do not delegate it.`,`Confirm age 18 or over and capacity for this decision.`,`Explain in the patient's language: purpose, **material risks**, alternatives, and no treatment.`,`Check understanding by teach-back and give time to decide.`,`Record on a **specific written form** and write the counselling in the notes.`,`Unexpected finding under anaesthesia: do only what prevents immediate danger.`,`For refusal or DAMA, record capacity, explanation and refusal on a signed form.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`In geriatrics, **speak to the patient first**, and to the family with permission.`,`Family **collusion** to hide a diagnosis is not a lawful instruction.`,`Elder abuse: interview alone, document, use the **Senior Citizens Act 2007** and Elderline 14567.`,`Telemedicine: consent is **implied if the patient initiates**, explicit if the doctor initiates.`,`MHCA Section 94 allows emergency treatment without consent for up to **72 hours**.`,`Sexual assault victims get free treatment in every hospital (**BNSS 397**, old CrPC 357C).`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`A signed form with no counselling note is treated as **uninformed consent**.`,`Extending an operation on a relative's consent is unlawful unless a true emergency.`,`Refuse record requests from relatives, employers or police without authority or due process.`,`Backdating or altering a record after an adverse event is fabrication.`,`Criminal negligence needs **gross negligence** (Jacob Mathew 2005); IPC 304A is now **BNS 106(1)**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Who consents in each situation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Who consents`,`Form`,`Authority`],rows:[[`Routine examination of an adult`,`The patient`,`Implied`,`Conduct`],[`Surgery, anaesthesia, transfusion`,`Patient 18+ with capacity`,`Written, specific, witnessed`,`BNS 26; Samira Kohli`],[`Child under 12 or unsound mind`,`Parent or guardian`,`Written`,`BNS 27 (IPC 89)`],[`Adult lacking capacity, not urgent`,`Guardian or nominated representative`,`Written with best-interests note`,`MHCA 2017 s4, s14`],[`Emergency, no one available`,`No consent needed`,`Document emergency and reasoning`,`BNS 30; Parmanand Katara`],[`Competent adult refusing`,`Refusal is respected`,`Record capacity; DAMA form`,`Unwise is not incapacity`],[`MTP`,`Woman 18+; guardian if minor`,`Written, prescribed form`,`MTP Act as amended 2021`]]},{heading:`Old and new legal sections`,columns:[`Subject`,`Old`,`New`,`Meaning for doctors`],rows:[[`Consent to risky act`,`IPC 87, 88`,`BNS 25, 26`,`Good-faith treatment with consent is protected`],[`Guardian consent; void consent`,`IPC 89, 90`,`BNS 27, 28`,`Under 12 or under fear is not consent`],[`Emergency; bad news in good faith`,`IPC 92, 93`,`BNS 30, 31`,`Doctrine of necessity`],[`Death by negligence`,`IPC 304A`,`BNS 106(1)`,`Doctor: up to 2 years and fine`],[`Rape victim exam; free treatment`,`CrPC 164A, 357C`,`BNSS 184, 397`,`Examine with consent; treat free`],[`Electronic records`,`IEA 65B`,`BSA 63`,`EMR admissible with certificate`]]},{heading:`Landmark cases`,columns:[`Case`,`Year`,`Holding`],rows:[[`Parmanand Katara`,`1989`,`Emergency care first, formalities later`],[`IMA v V.P. Shantha`,`1995`,`Paid medical care falls under consumer law`],[`Mr X v Hospital Z`,`1998`,`HIV disclosure to a person at real risk is no breach`],[`Jacob Mathew`,`2005`,`Criminal negligence needs gross negligence and expert opinion`],[`Samira Kohli`,`2008`,`Diagnostic consent does not cover therapy`],[`Puttaswamy`,`2017`,`Privacy, including health data, is a fundamental right`]]}],remember:[`The signature is not the consent; the counselling note is.`,`Samira Kohli: diagnostic consent is not therapeutic consent; no relative consent for a competent adult.`,`IPC 87-93 became BNS 25-31; quote both numbers.`,`Capacity: presumed, decision-specific, time-specific.`,`MHCA Section 4: understand, appreciate, communicate.`,`Records: keep 3 years minimum, copies within 72 hours.`,`Correct with a single dated line; never alter after a complaint.`]},updated:[`DPDP Rules 2025 (notified 13-14 November 2025): core data-fiduciary duties incl. security safeguards and breach notification apply 18 months later (May 2027); consent-manager rules after 12 months`,`Supreme Court 7 November 2024: three-judge bench declined the D.K. Gandhi reference as unnecessary - IMA v V.P. Shantha stands, doctors remain under consumer law`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Consent, capacity, confidentiality and records`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Valid consent = **competent person with capacity**, **voluntary**, **informed** (nature, purpose, material risks, alternatives, refusal), **specific**, given **before** the act, withdrawable. Consent is **consensus ad idem** (Contract Act s13), free if no coercion (s14).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Implied** (routine exam), **expressed oral**, **expressed written** (surgery, anaesthesia, transfusion, invasive tests, research, MLC), **proxy** (guardian/nominated representative). **Blanket consent is invalid.** Age: **18** for treatment; 12+ for examination; under 12 guardian.`],[`Law of consent (sections)`,`**IPC 87-93 = BNS 25-31** (from 1 July 2024): **BNS 26** good-faith act with consent; **BNS 27** guardian for under 12/unsound mind; **BNS 28** consent under fear/by child void; **BNS 30** emergency without consent (necessity); **BNS 31** good-faith communication.`],[`Key case law`,`**Samira Kohli 2008** - diagnostic consent does not cover therapy, **relative cannot consent for competent adult**; **Parmanand Katara 1989** - emergency care first; **Mr X v Hospital Z 1998** - disclosure to person at real risk; **Puttaswamy 2017** - privacy under Article 21.`],[`Capacity - the test`,`**MHCA 2017 s4**: **understand** information, **appreciate** foreseeable consequences, **communicate** decision (UK adds retain and weigh). Capacity is **presumed, decision-specific, time-specific**; **unwise decision is not incapacity**; **MMSE is not a capacity test**.`],[`Assessing capacity - steps`,`Correct reversible causes (delirium, pain, hypoxia) -> best time of day -> explain simply with interpreter, aids -> test each ability with open questions, record patient's words -> borderline: repeat or second opinion -> document.`],[`When capacity is absent`,`Non-emergency: **best interests** using past wishes, consult family or **nominated representative (MHCA s14)**, least restrictive option; emergency: **necessity (BNS 30)** - only what the emergency needs; no general Indian statute for proxy in physical illness (RPwD s14 limited guardianship).`],[`Confidentiality and exceptions`,`**IMC Reg 7.14** (2002 regs govern - NMC 2023 in abeyance). Exceptions: **consent**, **court order** (point out confidentiality first), **statute** (notifiable disease, RBD, **POCSO s19**), **serious identified risk** to another, incapacitated patient's interest. **HIV Act 2017** - disclosure only with consent or court order.`],[`Data protection`,`**DPDP Act 2023** - clinic is a **data fiduciary**: notice, consent, purpose limitation, safeguards, **breach notification**; **DPDP Rules 2025** phase duties in by **May 2027**. Never post clinical photographs without separate written consent.`],[`Medical records`,`**IMC Reg 1.3**: keep indoor records **3 years minimum**, copies **within 72 hours**, MLC register; keep longer (consumer limitation **2 years**, civil 3 years; children until majority + 3). **BSA 63** (old IEA 65B) - EMR admissible with certificate.`],[`Good record and corrections`,`Legible, **dated and timed**, signed with name and **registration number**, contemporaneous, relevant negatives, counselling in patient's words. Correct with **single line, dated, initialled**; after a complaint only a **labelled dated addendum**.`],[`Taking consent - procedure`,`Doctor who will perform takes it -> confirm 18+ and capacity -> explain in patient's language (risks, alternatives, no treatment) -> teach-back -> confirm voluntariness -> **specific form + counselling note** -> unexpected finding under GA: only what prevents immediate danger.`],[`Special groups`,`**Elderly** - talk to the patient, not the son; **collusion** is not a lawful instruction; **MTP** - woman's consent alone if 18+; **HIV test** - counselling + consent; **rape survivor** - BNSS 184 with consent, **BNSS 397 free treatment**; MHCA s94 - 72 h emergency treatment.`],[`Telemedicine`,`**Telemedicine Guidelines 2020**: consent **implied if patient initiates**, explicit if doctor initiates; verify identity; Lists O, A, B and prohibited (NDPS) drugs; same record duties.`],[`Consumer and criminal liability`,`**V.P. Shantha 1995** stands - 2024 reference **declined 7 Nov 2024**; CPA 2019 District up to **Rs 50 lakh**, State to **Rs 2 crore**, National above; **BNS 106(1)** (IPC 304A) - **up to 2 years** for an RMP; **Jacob Mathew** safeguards.`],[`Red flags`,`Signed form with no counselling note; extending surgery on a relative's consent; family collusion; records request without authorisation; MLC treatment delayed for police; pressure to alter notes; refusal without capacity assessment; signs of elder abuse.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**DPDP Rules 2025** notified (full duties May 2027); **V.P. Shantha reference declined (Nov 2024)**; BNS/BNSS/BSA in force since 1 July 2024; NMC 2023 regulations still in abeyance.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`The signature is not the consent - the note is. Capacity: presumed, decision-specific, time-specific. A relative cannot consent for a competent adult. Quote both old and new section numbers.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Consent decision at the bedside`,steps:[{label:`Life-threatening emergency, no one available?`,detail:`Yes - treat under necessity (BNS 30); record emergency and reasoning`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Patient 18+ - presume capacity`,detail:`Correct delirium, pain, hypoxia; best time of day; interpreter`},{label:`MHCA s4: understand, appreciate, communicate?`,detail:`Open questions; record answers in patient's words`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Has capacity - disclose and decide`,detail:`Material risks, alternatives, no treatment; refusal must be respected`,tone:`good`},{label:`Lacks capacity - best interests`,detail:`Advance directive, nominated representative or family; least restrictive option`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Document form plus counselling note`,detail:`Specific, signed, witnessed; the note proves informed consent`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Confidentiality - when disclosure is allowed`,root:`Duty of confidentiality (IMC Reg 7.14; Puttaswamy 2017)`,arms:[{label:`Consent`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Patient's valid consent`,`Relative has no automatic right`]},{label:`Court order`,steps:[`Point out confidentiality first`,`Then disclose what the court directs`]},{label:`Statute`,steps:[`Notifiable disease (TB - Nikshay)`,`RBD Act`,`POCSO s19 mandatory report`]},{label:`Serious risk to another`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Identified person at real risk`,`Mr X v Hospital Z 1998`]},{label:`Incapacitated patient`,steps:[`Need-to-know disclosure to carers and team`]}]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-dementia-caregiver":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Major neurocognitive disorder** (DSM-5-TR) is decline from baseline in one or more cognitive domains.`,`The decline must **interfere with independence** and not occur only in delirium.`,`**Mild cognitive impairment** is measurable decline with independence preserved.`,`MCI converts to dementia at about **10-15% a year**.`,`LASI-DAD: dementia in about **7.4% of Indians 60 and over**.`,`**Lancet 2024**: 14 modifiable risk factors explain about **45%** of dementia.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Alzheimer disease 50-60%**: gradual, memory first.`,`**Vascular 20-30%**: stepwise, early gait and urinary symptoms.`,`**Lewy body 5-10%**: fluctuation, visual hallucinations, parkinsonism.`,`**Frontotemporal**: behaviour or language first, usually under 65.`,`Mild stage: IADLs lost. Moderate: help with basic ADLs. Severe: fully dependent.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Amyloid-beta 42 forms extracellular **plaques** years before symptoms.`,`Hyperphosphorylated tau forms **neurofibrillary tangles**, which track symptoms better.`,`Early **hippocampal damage** explains loss of new memories first.`,`Loss of the nucleus basalis cuts acetylcholine; hence **cholinesterase inhibitors**.`,`**APOE e4** raises risk; Down syndrome almost always shows Alzheimer pathology by 40.`,`In Lewy body disease, few D2 receptors remain, causing **neuroleptic sensitivity**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**The informant history is the diagnostic test**; the score only stages it.`,`Note onset and course: gradual, stepwise, fluctuating, or behaviour first.`,`Ask about IADLs and ADLs; function separates dementia from MCI.`,`**Exclude delirium**: sudden onset, fluctuation and inattention.`,`**Exclude depression**: short history, patient complains more than family, says 'I do not know'.`,`Review drugs for anticholinergics and sedatives.`,`Ask about wandering, gas, driving, money, recent property signatures and carer coping.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Use tests valid for schooling: **Mini-Cog, MoCA, HMSE**; avoid unadjusted MMSE.`,`Screen delirium with the **4AT** and depression with the **GDS-15**.`,`Look for focal signs, parkinsonism, vertical gaze palsy and primitive reflexes.`,`A magnetic, shuffling gait suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus or small-vessel disease.`,`Check **pulse** before a cholinesterase inhibitor, plus lying and standing BP.`,`Check hearing and vision, and score pain with **PAINAD** in advanced disease.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Bloods: haemogram, glucose, renal function, **sodium, calcium, TSH, B12 and folate**.`,`HIV and syphilis serology if risk factors or atypical course.`,`CT or MRI for onset under 65, rapid decline, focal signs or early gait problems.`,`**ECG** before a cholinesterase inhibitor or antipsychotic.`,`Urine culture only with urinary symptoms or fever.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Disclose the diagnosis in a planned way and **review within 2 weeks**.`,`Use routine, orientation cues, good light and **cognitive stimulation therapy**.`,`Teach carers: one instruction at a time, do not argue with delusions.`,`Behaviour: use **DICE**: Describe, Investigate, Create a plan, Evaluate.`,`Make home safe: gas cut-off, hidden keys, an identity card for wandering.`,`Do legal planning early: **will, power of attorney, advance directive**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Mild-moderate Alzheimer, Lewy body or Parkinson dementia: **cholinesterase inhibitor**.`,`Moderate-severe Alzheimer: **memantine**, alone or added on.`,`No cholinesterase inhibitor in **MCI or frontotemporal dementia**.`,`Vascular dementia: control BP, diabetes, lipids and AF.`,`Antipsychotics only for severe risk; **risperidone 0.25 mg BD**, up to 6 weeks.`,`**Never give haloperidol in Lewy body dementia**.`,`Depression in dementia: **sertraline**, not tricyclics; check sodium at 2-4 weeks.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**The caregiver is a patient too**; burden predicts institutional care.`,`Score burden with the **Zarit interview**; over 40 is moderate-severe.`,`Education, skills training, respite and support groups work.`,`Senior Citizens Act 2007 **Section 23** can void a property gift if care is denied.`,`Give **Elderline 14567** and the ARDSI contact to every family.`,`Advanced dementia: **tube feeding does not prolong life**; offer careful hand feeding.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review 4-6 weeks after starting a cholinesterase inhibitor, then every 6 months.`,`Review antipsychotics every **6-12 weeks** with a planned taper.`,`Refer urgently for rapid decline, onset under 65, seizures or myoclonus.`,`Anti-amyloid antibodies (lecanemab, donanemab) are **memory-clinic only**, for MCI or mild Alzheimer.`,`A carer who cannot cope or talks of harm needs **same-day** action.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in dementia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Donepezil`,`5 mg at night, then 10 mg after 4-6 weeks`,`While benefit continues`,`Bradycardia, heart block; pulse and ECG first`],[`Rivastigmine`,`Oral 1.5 mg BD to 6 mg BD; patch 4.6 then 9.5 mg`,`While benefit continues`,`GI upset; restart low if missed over 3 days`],[`Galantamine ER`,`8 mg daily, then 16 mg (max 24)`,`While benefit continues`,`Avoid in severe renal or liver disease`],[`Memantine`,`5 mg daily, up 5 mg weekly to 20 mg`,`Long term`,`Dizziness; 10 mg if CrCl 5-29`],[`Risperidone`,`0.25 mg BD, max 1 mg BD`,`Up to 6 weeks`,`Stroke, death; never in Lewy body`],[`Sertraline`,`25 mg daily, up to 50-100 mg`,`As for depression`,`Hyponatraemia; check sodium at 2-4 weeks`],[`Melatonin PR`,`2 mg at bedtime; 3-12 mg for RBD`,`As needed`,`Morning drowsiness`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g every 6 hours`,`Trial when pain suspected`,`Max 3 g a day if frail`]]},{heading:`Types of dementia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Alzheimer`,`Vascular`,`Lewy body`,`Frontotemporal`],rows:[[`Course`,`Gradual over years`,`Stepwise`,`Fluctuating`,`Progressive, often under 65`],[`First symptom`,`Episodic memory loss`,`Slowing, gait, urgency`,`Attention, hallucinations`,`Behaviour or language`],[`Signs`,`Normal neurology early`,`Focal signs`,`Parkinsonism, REM sleep disorder`,`Disinhibition, hyperorality`],[`Drug`,`Cholinesterase inhibitor, memantine`,`Vascular risk control`,`Cholinesterase inhibitor works well`,`SSRI; no cholinesterase inhibitor`],[`Avoid`,`Anticholinergics`,`Uncontrolled BP`,`Antipsychotics`,`Cholinesterase inhibitors`]]},{heading:`Delirium, dementia and depression`,columns:[`Feature`,`Delirium`,`Dementia`,`Depression`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Hours to days`,`Months to years`,`Weeks`],[`Course`,`Fluctuates, worse at night`,`Slowly progressive`,`Worse in the morning`],[`Attention`,`Impaired, cardinal sign`,`Clear until late`,`Clear but poor effort`],[`Memory complaint`,`Not voiced`,`Family reports it`,`Patient emphasises it`],[`Tool`,`4AT or CAM`,`Mini-Cog, MoCA, HMSE`,`GDS-15`]]}],remember:[`The informant diagnoses dementia; the score only stages it.`,`Exclude delirium and depression first; all three can coexist.`,`Fluctuation, hallucinations, parkinsonism: put the antipsychotic pen down.`,`DICE before drugs: Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate.`,`Never treat donepezil urgency with oxybutynin.`,`Zarit over 40 means the carer is close to collapse.`,`Legal planning while capacity remains.`]},updated:[`CDSCO 2025-26: donanemab (approved November 2025) and lecanemab (launched 2026) now available in India for MCI or mild dementia due to Alzheimer disease with confirmed amyloid - memory-clinic use with MRI monitoring for ARIA`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Dementia and caregiver support`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Major neurocognitive disorder (DSM-5-TR)**: decline from baseline in **1 or more of 6 domains** (complex attention, executive, learning-memory, language, perceptual-motor, social cognition), concern + objective testing, **interferes with independence**, not only in delirium. **MCI** = independence preserved (10-15%/year convert).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Alzheimer 50-60%**, vascular 20-30%, mixed, **Lewy body 5-10%**, frontotemporal (commonest under 65), reversible causes. Severity: **mild** (IADL), **moderate** (ADL help, BPSD), **severe** (dependent, dysphagia).`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`**Lancet Commission 2024 - 14 modifiable factors, about 45%**: education, **hearing loss**, hypertension, obesity, smoking, depression, inactivity, diabetes, alcohol, head injury, air pollution, isolation, **untreated vision loss, high LDL**. APOE e4; Down syndrome. India - **LASI-DAD 7.4% of 60+**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`APP -> beta/gamma-secretase -> **amyloid-beta 42 plaques** -> **tau tangles** (Braak) -> synaptic loss -> **nucleus basalis cholinergic deficit**; DLB - alpha-synuclein, deep cholinergic loss, **neuroleptic sensitivity**; FTD - tau/TDP-43, no cholinergic deficit.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Informant history is the diagnostic test** (AD8 2 or more, IQCODE); onset and course (gradual, stepwise, fluctuating); IADL/ADL; **exclude delirium** (sudden, inattention) and **depression**; hallucinations, RBD (DLB); disinhibition (FTD); gait + incontinence (NPH); drugs; risk; carer.`],[`Key examination`,`**Mini-Cog (2 or less), MoCA (under 26), HMSE** for low literacy, ACE-III; **4AT**, GDS-15; focal signs, parkinsonism, vertical gaze palsy, primitive reflexes; gait; lying-standing BP; **pulse before ChEI**; hearing and vision; PAINAD in advanced disease.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`CBC, glucose/HbA1c, renal, **Na, Ca**, LFT, **TSH, B12, folate**; HIV/VDRL if risk or atypical; **CT/MRI** if under 65, rapid, focal, head injury, anticoagulant, early gait/incontinence; **ECG** before ChEI or antipsychotic; urine only if symptoms.`],[`Treatment - general`,`Planned disclosure, review 2 weeks; routine, orientation cues, **cognitive stimulation therapy**; communication (one instruction, do not argue); **DICE** for behaviour; home safety, ID card; exercise, hearing aids; **legal planning while capacity remains**; no tube feeding in advanced dementia.`],[`Treatment - first line (drugs)`,`**Donepezil 5 mg at night x 4-6 weeks -> 10 mg**; **rivastigmine patch 4.6 -> 9.5 mg/24 h** (oral 1.5 -> 6 mg BD); **galantamine ER 8 -> 16-24 mg** - for mild-moderate AD, **DLB and PDD**. Side effects: GI, **bradycardia, heart block, syncope**, vivid dreams, urgency.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Memantine 5 mg -> 20 mg daily** (10 mg if CrCl 5-29) for moderate AD intolerant of ChEI, severe AD, or add-on. **Not in MCI or FTD**. Anti-amyloid antibodies (**donanemab, lecanemab - CDSCO approved**) only for MCI/mild AD with confirmed amyloid in memory clinics (ARIA, MRI monitoring, cost).`],[`BPSD`,`Non-drug first (DICE - pain, constipation, retention, infection, boredom). **Risperidone 0.25 mg BD, max 1 mg BD, up to 6 weeks** only for severe distress/risk; **review 6-12 weeks, taper**; risk **1.6-1.7x death**, stroke. **No haloperidol in Lewy body**. Depression - sertraline 25-100 mg; sleep - melatonin 2 mg, trazodone 25-50 mg.`],[`Special groups`,`**DLB** - ChEI works well, avoid antipsychotics, melatonin for RBD; **vascular** - control BP, diabetes, lipids, AF; **FTD** - SSRI for behaviour; **NPH** - tap test and shunt; **advanced** - palliative approach, careful hand feeding.`],[`Caregiver`,`**Zarit Burden Interview**: 0-20 little, 21-40 mild-moderate, **41-60 moderate-severe**, 61-88 severe; screen carer for depression (PHQ-9); **education + skills training + respite + support groups (REACH-type)** work; ARDSI, day care, Elderline 14567.`],[`Law and protection`,`Will, power of attorney (1882 Act), **advance directive (Common Cause 2023)**, **MHCA s14 nominated representative**; no statutory health LPA in India; **MWPSC Act 2007** - tribunal maintenance, **s23 - Tribunal can void a property transfer made on promise of care**, s24 abandonment punishable.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Sudden worsening (delirium); rapid decline over weeks with myoclonus (prion, autoimmune); onset under 65; focal signs; early gait + incontinence (NPH); neuroleptic sensitivity; carer threatening harm; abuse or property transfer; wandering, gas fires.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **2 weeks** after diagnosis, **4-6 weeks** after ChEI start (pulse, weight, GI), then **6-monthly** (function, behaviour, carer, safety, drugs); antipsychotic every 6-12 weeks with taper plan.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Modify the 14 Lancet 2024 risk factors** across life - hearing aids, BP, LDL, vision, education, activity, no smoking; NPHCE memory clinics, **Tele-MANAS 14416**, ARDSI dementia day care, **Elderline 14567**; Dementia India Strategy (ARDSI 2018).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Donanemab (Nov 2025) and lecanemab (2026) approved/launched in India** for early AD with amyloid confirmation; Lancet Commission 2024 added vision loss and LDL (14 factors); NICE NG97 unchanged for ChEI/memantine.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`The informant diagnoses, the test stages. Fluctuation + hallucinations + parkinsonism - stop before an antipsychotic. ChEI do not help FTD. Never treat donepezil urgency with oxybutynin. Plan legally while capacity remains.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Memory complaint in the OPD - diagnostic path`,steps:[{label:`Sudden onset, fluctuating, inattentive?`,detail:`4AT 4 or more - delirium: emergency, find cause`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Low mood, 'I do not know' answers?`,detail:`GDS-15 5 or more - treat depression, then retest`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Informant history + cognitive test`,detail:`AD8 2 or more; Mini-Cog 2 or less, MoCA under 26, HMSE if unschooled`},{label:`Independence lost? Dementia vs MCI`,detail:`MCI - no ChEI; review yearly`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Bloods, ECG; image if atypical`,detail:`TSH, B12, Na, Ca; CT/MRI if under 65, rapid, focal, gait + incontinence`},{label:`Type it and plan`,detail:`AD/DLB/PDD - ChEI; FTD - SSRI; vascular - risk factors; carer and legal plan`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Drug ladder in Alzheimer disease and BPSD`,steps:[{label:`Non-drug care and DICE first`,detail:`Treat pain (paracetamol 1 g 6-hourly trial), constipation, retention, infection`},{label:`Cholinesterase inhibitor (mild-moderate)`,detail:`Donepezil 5 -> 10 mg at night; rivastigmine patch 4.6 -> 9.5 mg/24 h; pulse/ECG first`},{label:`Add or switch to memantine (moderate-severe)`,detail:`5 mg weekly steps to 20 mg daily; 10 mg if CrCl 5-29`},{label:`Antipsychotic only for severe risk`,detail:`Risperidone 0.25 mg BD, max 1 mg BD, 6 weeks; never haloperidol in DLB`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Early AD with amyloid - memory clinic`,detail:`Lecanemab/donanemab (CDSCO approved) with MRI for ARIA; refer, not start`,tone:`good`}]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-frailty-sarcopenia-nutrition":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Frailty** is loss of reserve across several systems, so a small stress causes a big fall in function.`,`Frailty is not the same as multimorbidity, disability or old age.`,`**Sarcopenia** is a generalised loss of muscle strength and mass.`,`**Cachexia** is inflammatory wasting that feeding alone cannot reverse.`,`Malnutrition in the old: weight loss **over 5% in 6 months** or **BMI under 22**.`,`Frailty is dynamic; even people in their 90s respond to training and protein.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Fried phenotype**: weight loss, exhaustion, weak grip, slow gait, low activity.`,`Fried: **3 or more of 5 is frail**, 1-2 pre-frail, 0 robust.`,`**FRAIL scale**: Fatigue, Resistance, Ambulation, Illnesses, Loss of weight; 3 or more is frail.`,`**Clinical Frailty Scale 5 or more** is frail.`,`**AWGS 2025** (India): sarcopenia is **low strength plus low muscle mass**, from age 50.`,`In AWGS 2025, gait speed is an outcome, and the 'severe' category is dropped.`,`MNA-SF: **0-7 malnourished**, 8-11 at risk, 12-14 normal.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Motor neurons die with age, leaving fewer, slower motor units.`,`**Type II fast fibres** shrink, so the quick step that saves a trip is lost.`,`**Anabolic resistance**: older muscle needs 25-30 g protein per meal to build.`,`Inflammageing (IL-6, TNF-alpha) and falling hormones increase muscle breakdown.`,`**10 days of bed rest** removes years' worth of leg strength.`,`Fried's cycle: poor intake, weak muscle, less activity, less appetite, poorer intake.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about weight loss, exhaustion, stairs, walking a block and falls.`,`Take a diet history: protein sources, who cooks, eating alone, money for food.`,`Use **MEALS ON WHEELS** to search for causes of weight loss.`,`Ask about **teeth, dentures** and swallowing; tooth loss is a common reversible cause.`,`Ask about TB and cancer symptoms: cough, night sweats, lumps, bleeding.`,`Review drugs that reduce appetite: metformin, digoxin, SSRIs, opioids, donepezil.`,`Ask about old restrictive diets that are no longer useful.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Weigh every visit and plot the trend**; one weight is almost useless.`,`**Calf circumference** under 34 cm (men) or 33 cm (women) suggests low muscle.`,`Grip under **28 kg (men) or 18 kg (women)** at 65 or over is low strength.`,`At 50-64, the grip cut-offs are **34 kg and 20 kg**.`,`Do the 5-times chair stand and gait speed to track progress.`,`Examine the mouth, look for deficiency signs, and do a water swallow test.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Haemogram, CRP, glucose, creatinine, calcium, liver tests and TSH.`,`**Albumin reflects illness, not nutrition**; do not use it to monitor feeding.`,`Vitamin B12, 25-OH vitamin D and ferritin where relevant.`,`Weight loss over 5%: chest X-ray and sputum NAAT if cough.`,`Refeeding risk: check **phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily for 3 days**.`,`DXA is not needed to start treatment; low strength is enough.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Progressive resistance exercise** is the most effective intervention.`,`Do 2-3 sessions a week, 2-3 sets of **8-12 repetitions**, raising the load.`,`Home moves: sit-to-stand, wall push-ups, heel raises, step-ups, bands.`,`Protein **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day** if well, **1.2-1.5 g/kg/day** if ill.`,`Spread protein as 25-30 g at each meal: dal, curd, milk, eggs, paneer, soya.`,`Fortify food first with ghee, milk powder or ground nuts.`,`**Remove needless low-salt, low-fat diets** in the thin frail patient.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**There is no approved drug for sarcopenia**.`,`Oral supplements: **at least 400 kcal and 30 g protein daily**, between meals, for 1 month.`,`Vitamin D under 20 ng/mL: **cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks**.`,`**Avoid megestrol**: it adds fat and clots, not muscle (Beers).`,`Mirtazapine only if depression coexists, not as an appetite drug.`,`Relax targets in frailty: HbA1c under 8.0%, avoid hypoglycaemia.`,`Treat osteoporosis, as frail patients are at high fracture risk.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Weight loss over 5% in 6 months needs a search for TB, cancer and thyroid disease.`,`Wet voice or coughing at meals means aspiration risk.`,`Rapid loss of function over days is acute illness, not frailty.`,`**Refeeding syndrome**: low phosphate, potassium and magnesium in the first 72 hours.`,`Refeeding risk: give **thiamine before food** and start at **10 kcal/kg/day**.`,`A pressure ulcer in an undernourished patient is a sentinel event.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review **monthly** on supplements: weight, intake, grip and MNA-SF.`,`Use the CFS to tailor decisions, **never as a blanket rationing ceiling**.`,`CFS 7-9 suggests limited life; start advance care planning.`,`Refer to physiotherapy, dietitian, speech therapist and dentist as needed.`,`Link to old-age pension, Annapurna grain scheme and **PM-JAY Vay Vandana**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and supplements in frailty`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Oral nutritional supplement`,`400 kcal and 30 g protein daily`,`At least 1 month`,`Between meals; bloating`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU oral weekly`,`8 weeks, then 800-2000 IU daily`,`No annual megadoses; they increase falls`],[`Calcium carbonate`,`500 mg elemental with meals`,`Long term, to 1000-1200 mg total`,`Constipation; separate from iron`],[`Vitamin B12`,`1000-2000 microgram oral daily`,`Recheck at 8 weeks`,`IM hydroxocobalamin 1 mg if neuro signs`],[`Ferrous sulphate`,`200 mg once daily or alternate days`,`3 months after Hb normal`,`Constipation; avoid tea and calcium`],[`Folic acid`,`5 mg oral daily`,`4 months`,`Only after B12 deficiency excluded`],[`Thiamine (refeeding)`,`200-300 mg oral daily`,`First 10 days of feeding`,`Give before feeding starts`],[`Mirtazapine`,`7.5-15 mg at night`,`Review at 4-6 weeks`,`Only with depression; sedation, falls`]]},{heading:`Sarcopenia criteria - AWGS 2025 and EWGSOP2`,columns:[`Measure`,`AWGS 2025 (India)`,`EWGSOP2 (Europe)`],rows:[[`Case finding`,`Calf under 34 or 33 cm; SARC-F 4+`,`SARC-F 4 or more`],[`Grip strength`,`Under 28 or 18 kg at 65+`,`Under 27 or 16 kg`],[`Grip at 50-64`,`Under 34 or 20 kg`,`Not defined`],[`Diagnosis`,`Low strength plus low mass`,`Low strength plus low mass`],[`Performance`,`Outcome measure only`,`Low performance makes it severe`]]},{heading:`Clinical Frailty Scale (short form)`,columns:[`Score`,`Category`,`Key feature`],rows:[[`1-3`,`Very fit to managing well`,`Independent, active`],[`4`,`Very mild frailty`,`Slowed up, symptoms limit activity`],[`5`,`Mild frailty`,`Help with finances, transport, heavy housework`],[`6`,`Moderate frailty`,`Help outside, with stairs and bathing`],[`7`,`Severe frailty`,`Dependent for personal care, stable`],[`8`,`Very severe frailty`,`Dependent, approaching end of life`],[`9`,`Terminally ill`,`Life expectancy under 6 months`]]}],remember:[`FRAIL: Fatigue, Resistance, Ambulation, Illnesses, Loss of weight.`,`Fried and FRAIL: 3 or more of 5 is frail.`,`Strength, not mass, is the primary sarcopenia measure.`,`Older people need more protein, not less: 25-30 g per meal.`,`No drug for sarcopenia; resistance exercise is the treatment.`,`Megestrol adds fat and clots, not muscle.`,`Thiamine before food; start refeeding at 10 kcal/kg/day.`,`Albumin measures illness, not nutrition.`]},updated:[`AWGS 2025 (Nature Aging, Nov 2025) replaces AWGS 2019: sarcopenia = low muscle strength plus low muscle mass; extended to age 50-64`,`AWGS 2025: grip under 28 / 18 kg at 65 or over and under 34 / 20 kg at 50-64; ASM/BMI added as a muscle index`,`AWGS 2025: physical performance (gait speed, chair stand, SPPB) becomes an outcome measure and the severe sarcopenia category is dropped`,`ADA 2026: HbA1c under 8.0% in frail/complex older adults (replaces 7.5-8.5%)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Frailty, sarcopenia and nutrition`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Frailty** - loss of reserve across systems so a minor stressor causes a large fall in function (not = multimorbidity or old age). **Sarcopenia** - progressive generalised loss of muscle **strength and mass**. **Malnutrition** - inadequate intake, reversible; **cachexia** - inflammatory, not reversed by feeding.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Fried phenotype** (weight loss 4.5 kg/5%, exhaustion, weak grip, slow gait, low activity) - **3+ frail, 1-2 pre-frail**; **Rockwood FI 0.25+**, **CFS 5+ frail**; **FRAIL scale** 3+; **AWGS 2025**: at risk - possible sarcopenia - sarcopenia (**low strength + low mass**); EWGSOP2: probable - confirmed - severe.`],[`Diagnostic cut-offs (India)`,`**AWGS 2025**: grip **under 28 kg men / 18 kg women at 65+**, **under 34 / 20 kg at 50-64**; mass ASM/ht2 (DXA under 7.0/5.4, BIA under 7.0/5.7 kg/m2) or **ASM/BMI**; case finding (AWGS 2019 tools) **calf under 34/33 cm**, **SARC-F 4+**; gait speed and SPPB now **outcome measures**, no severe category.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`Age, inactivity, **bed rest (10 days = years of loss)**, poor protein intake, poverty, tooth loss, depression, dementia, drugs, chronic disease (TB, cancer, COPD, CKD), vitamin D deficiency, widowhood, living alone.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Motor unit loss + **type II fibre atrophy** + fewer satellite cells -> **anabolic resistance** (needs leucine 2.5-3 g/meal) + **inflammageing** (IL-6, TNF) + falling anabolic hormones -> sarcopenia -> **Fried cycle** (less activity -> less appetite -> undernutrition).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Weight loss (amount, speed); exhaustion; stairs, block, chair rise, carrying 5 kg (SARC-F); falls; **diet history and who cooks, money for food**; **MEALS ON WHEELS** causes; teeth and swallowing; TB/cancer red flags; drugs causing anorexia; mood, memory.`],[`Key examination`,`**Weight trend**, BMI (concern **under 22** in elderly); **calf circumference**; **grip strength** (best of 2-3); **5 chair stands** (12 s or more); gait speed/SPPB to track; **CFS** at baseline 2 weeks before illness; mouth; deficiency signs; water swallow test.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`CBC, CRP, glucose, renal, Ca, LFT, TSH; **albumin = illness marker, not nutrition**; B12, 25-OH D, ferritin; CXR + sputum NAAT if cough; **PO4, K, Mg daily x 3 days** if refeeding risk; DXA/BIA confirms but **low strength is enough to act**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Progressive resistance exercise 2-3 times/week, 2-3 sets of 8-12 reps** + balance; **protein 1.0-1.2 g/kg/day (1.2-1.5 if ill)**, **25-30 g per meal**; energy about **30 kcal/kg/day**; fortify foods (dal, curd, milk, eggs, paneer, soya); remove restrictive diets; dentures.`],[`Supplements and drugs`,`**ONS 400 kcal + 30 g protein/day between meals x 1 month+**; **cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly x 8, then 800-2000 IU daily**; calcium to 1000-1200 mg; **B12 1000-2000 microgram oral daily**; **ferrous sulphate 200 mg alternate days**; **no approved drug for sarcopenia**; **avoid megestrol** (Beers); mirtazapine only with depression.`],[`Refeeding syndrome`,`Risk: **BMI under 16**, weight loss over 15% in 3-6 months, little intake over 10 days, low K/PO4/Mg -> **thiamine 200-300 mg before feeding x 10 days**, start **10 kcal/kg/day** (5 if extreme), build over 4-7 days, replace electrolytes daily.`],[`Special groups`,`**Diabetes - HbA1c under 8.0%** (ADA 2026), avoid sulfonylureas, SGLT2i/GLP-1 can worsen weight loss; **CKD** - caution with 2 g/kg protein; **sarcopenic obesity** - fat hides lost muscle; **pre-surgery** - 4-6 weeks prehabilitation.`],[`Complications`,`Falls, fractures, disability, delirium, **pressure injury** (sentinel of malnutrition), infection, hospital admission, institutionalisation, death; refeeding arrhythmia and Wernicke encephalopathy.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Weight loss over 5% in 6 months with TB/cancer symptoms; dysphagia with wet voice; rapid loss of function (illness, not frailty); refeeding-risk patient (admit); weight loss despite food available (depression, dental, neglect).`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`**Monthly** weight, intake, grip/chair stand, MNA-SF while on supplements; repeat **CFS after admission**; yearly rescreen (3-monthly in care homes); CFS 7-9 - start advance care planning.`],[`Prevention / national programme`,`**NPHCE**, Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; **IGNOAPS** pension, **Annapurna** grain, PDS; **Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana** dentures; **PM-JAY Vay Vandana** 70+; WHO activity 150-300 min/week + strength 3 days.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AWGS 2025**: diagnosis = low strength + low mass from age 50 (grip under 34/20 kg at 50-64), performance as outcome, severe category dropped; **ADA 2026** HbA1c under 8.0% in complex older adults.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Strength, not mass, is the primary sarcopenia measure. No drug for sarcopenia - resistance exercise + protein. Older people need more protein, not less. Albumin measures illness. Thiamine before food.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Sarcopenia in Indian primary care - AWGS 2025`,caption:`Two diagnostic components only; performance tracks outcome.`,steps:[{label:`Case find from age 50`,detail:`SARC-F 4 or more or calf under 34 cm (men) / 33 cm (women)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Measure grip strength`,detail:`Under 28/18 kg at 65+, under 34/20 kg at 50-64 = possible sarcopenia`},{label:`Act now - do not wait for DXA`,detail:`Resistance exercise, protein 1.2 g/kg, vitamin D if deficient, find causes`},{label:`Measure muscle mass where available`,detail:`ASM/ht2 by DXA or BIA, or ASM/BMI`},{label:`Low strength + low mass = sarcopenia`,detail:`No 'severe' grade in AWGS 2025`,tone:`good`},{label:`Track outcome with performance`,detail:`Gait speed, 5 chair stands, SPPB at each review`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Frailty and sarcopenia tools - what each measures`,columns:[`Tool`,`Items`,`Cut-off`],rows:[[`Fried phenotype`,`Weight loss, exhaustion, grip, gait, activity`,`3+ frail, 1-2 pre-frail`],[`FRAIL scale`,`Fatigue, Resistance, Ambulation, Illnesses, Loss of weight`,`3+ frail, 1-2 pre-frail`],[`Clinical Frailty Scale`,`Judgement of function 1-9`,`5+ frail; 7-9 start ACP`],[`Frailty Index`,`Deficits present / counted`,`0.25 or more frail`],[`AWGS 2025 sarcopenia`,`Grip + muscle mass`,`Grip under 28/18 kg (65+), under 34/20 kg (50-64)`],[`EWGSOP2 sarcopenia`,`Grip, mass, performance`,`Grip under 27/16 kg; severe if gait 0.8 m/s or less`],[`MNA-SF`,`Intake, weight, mobility, stress, neuropsych, BMI`,`0-7 malnourished, 8-11 at risk`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Nutrition support ladder in the frail elder`,steps:[{label:`Treat causes and remove restrictive diets`,detail:`Teeth, depression, drugs, poverty, dysphagia, TB, cancer`},{label:`Food first - fortify and spread protein`,detail:`25-30 g protein x 3 meals; dal, curd, milk, eggs, paneer, soya`},{label:`Oral nutritional supplement`,detail:`400 kcal + 30 g protein/day between meals, at least 1 month, monthly review`},{label:`Correct deficiencies`,detail:`Vitamin D 60,000 IU weekly x 8; B12 1000-2000 microgram; iron alternate days`},{label:`Severe malnutrition - admit, refeed safely`,detail:`Thiamine first; 10 kcal/kg/day; PO4, K, Mg daily x 3 days`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-pressure-ulcers":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **pressure injury** is localised skin and tissue damage over a bony prominence or under a device.`,`It is caused by sustained **pressure, or pressure plus shear**.`,`NPIAP renamed ulcer to **injury** in 2016; both terms are accepted.`,`Commonest sites: **sacrum and heels** lying, trochanter on the side, ischia sitting.`,`It is largely preventable; a **Kennedy terminal ulcer** in the dying may be unavoidable.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Stage 1**: non-blanchable erythema of intact skin.`,`**Stage 2**: partial-thickness loss with exposed pink dermis or blister.`,`**Stage 3**: full-thickness skin loss; fat visible.`,`**Stage 4**: bone, tendon, muscle or cartilage exposed.`,`**Unstageable**: base hidden by slough or eschar. **Deep tissue injury**: persistent maroon or purple area.`,`**Staging never goes backwards**; write 'healing stage 4'.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Capillary closing pressure is about 32 mmHg**; a mattress puts 70-100 mmHg on the sacrum.`,`Irreversible change starts after about **2 hours**; hence 2-hourly turning.`,`Pressure is highest at the bone-muscle interface, so damage starts deep: the **iceberg effect**.`,`**Shear** occurs when the bed head is raised above 30 degrees and the body slides down.`,`Moisture from urine and sweat macerates skin and multiplies friction.`,`Reperfusion injury lets a stage 1 deepen over 48-72 hours.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask how the wound started; a long lie or long operation suggests deep injury.`,`Ask who turns the patient and how often; **immobility** is the strongest risk factor.`,`Ask about urine and faecal incontinence and how fast pads are changed.`,`Take a diet history: protein, appetite, chewing, weight loss.`,`Ask about fever, foul smell, spreading redness and new confusion.`,`Ask what the family has applied, and about carer fatigue and goals of care.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Inspect **all pressure points** and the skin under every device.`,`**Blanch test**: non-blanchable redness is stage 1.`,`In dark skin, look for **warmth, induration, pain or a purple hue**.`,`Measure length, width and depth; record undermining and photograph with a ruler.`,`**Probe-to-bone** positive means likely osteomyelitis.`,`Check pulses and **ABPI** before debriding heel or leg ulcers.`,`Score **Braden**: 18 or less at risk, 9 or less very high risk.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Haemoglobin and blood glucose for every patient.`,`**Culture only clinically infected wounds**, from deep tissue after debridement.`,`A surface swab grows colonisers and misleads.`,`Suspected osteomyelitis: X-ray first, **MRI** best, bone biopsy gold standard.`,`**ESR over 70 mm/h** in a non-healing stage 4 suggests osteomyelitis.`,`Biopsy a heaped, everted edge to exclude **Marjolin ulcer**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Use the **SSKIN bundle**: Surface, Skin inspection, Keep moving, Incontinence, Nutrition.`,`**Turn at least every 2 hours** in bed and every hour in a chair.`,`Use a **30-degree tilt** and keep the bed head at 30 degrees or less.`,`**Float the heels** with a pillow under the calves.`,`**No donut rings and no massage** of red skin.`,`Give **30-35 kcal/kg/day** and protein **1.25-1.5 g/kg/day**.`,`Train the carer with a turning chart by the bed.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Follow **TIME**: Tissue debridement, Infection control, Moisture balance, Edge advancement.`,`Clean with saline or boiled cooled water; avoid peroxide, povidone and eusol on granulation.`,`Stage 2: **hydrocolloid**. Exuding cavity: **alginate** or foam. Dry slough: **hydrogel**.`,`**Leave stable dry heel eschar alone** when perfusion is poor.`,`Systemic antibiotics only for **cellulitis, osteomyelitis or sepsis**.`,`Use an **alternating-pressure air mattress** for Braden 12 or less or stage 3-4.`,`NPWT at -125 mmHg helps clean deep ulcers; not over osteomyelitis or eschar.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fever, hypotension or new confusion means **sepsis**: admit.`,`Crepitus and rapidly spreading necrosis suggest **necrotising infection**.`,`**Osteomyelitis** needs about 6 weeks of biopsy-guided antibiotics plus debridement.`,`Black heel with absent pulses means critical ischaemia; do not debride.`,`Multiple severe ulcers with poor hygiene raise suspicion of **elder neglect**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Re-score Braden at least weekly; measure and photograph the wound weekly.`,`Stage 3-4 not shrinking **20-40% in 2-4 weeks**: reassess everything.`,`Refer to surgery for debridement, abscess, osteomyelitis or flap closure.`,`Flaps fail (30-60% recurrence) unless nutrition and off-loading are fixed.`,`**Document stage, size and photographs** every visit; records are the main defence.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and dressings`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Hydrocolloid`,`Stage 2, 2-3 cm overlap`,`Change every 3-5 days`,`Not on infected or deep wounds`],[`Calcium alginate`,`Loosely fill exuding cavity`,`Change daily to every 2-3 days`,`Sticks to dry wounds`],[`Hydrogel`,`3-5 mm layer on dry slough`,`Change every 1-3 days`,`Peri-wound maceration`],[`Collagenase ointment`,`2 mm layer once daily`,`2-4 weeks`,`Inactivated by silver or iodine`],[`Silver sulfadiazine 1%`,`Thin layer once daily`,`2-week challenge`,`Sulfa allergy; leucopenia`],[`Metronidazole gel 0.75-1%`,`Once or twice daily`,`5-7 days`,`For odour; local irritation`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`5-7 days for cellulitis`,`Deep culture first; diarrhoea`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV every 6-8 hours`,`Sepsis, then by culture`,`Add vancomycin if MRSA likely`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g oral every 6 hours`,`30-60 min before dressings`,`Max 3 g if frail or under 50 kg`],[`Arginine-zinc ONS`,`1-2 servings daily`,`At least 8 weeks`,`Between meals`]]},{heading:`NPIAP stages`,columns:[`Stage`,`Appearance`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Stage 1`,`Non-blanchable redness, intact skin`,`Off-load; film or silicone foam`],[`Stage 2`,`Pink dermis or serum blister`,`Hydrocolloid or thin foam`],[`Stage 3`,`Fat visible, slough possible`,`Debride; alginate or foam; consider NPWT`],[`Stage 4`,`Bone, tendon or muscle exposed`,`Exclude osteomyelitis; refer for flap`],[`Unstageable`,`Base covered by slough or eschar`,`Debride to stage; spare dry heel eschar`],[`Deep tissue injury`,`Maroon or purple area, blood blister`,`Off-load; watch 72 hours`]]},{heading:`Support surfaces`,columns:[`Device`,`Best for`,`Common error`],rows:[[`High-specification foam mattress`,`Everyone at risk (Braden 18 or less)`,`Thinking turning is no longer needed`],[`Alternating-pressure air mattress`,`Braden 12 or less, stage 3-4`,`Pump switched off at night`],[`Seat cushion`,`Chair users`,`Donut ring, sitting all day`],[`Heel boot or calf pillow`,`Bedbound, heel injury`,`Pillow under the heels`],[`Home foam (4-5 inch)`,`When an air mattress is unaffordable`,`Plastic sheet on top`]]}],remember:[`SSKIN: Surface, Skin inspection, Keep moving, Incontinence, Nutrition.`,`TIME: Tissue, Infection, Moisture, Edge.`,`Off-loading heals; dressings only manage the bed.`,`Iceberg: damage starts deep, skin shows the tip.`,`Braden 18 or less at risk; 9 or less very high risk.`,`Head end 30 degrees or less, 30-degree tilt, heels floating.`,`Never a surface swab; deep tissue after debridement.`]},updated:[`International Guideline 4th edition (EPUAP/NPIAP/PPPIA) launched online February 2025 as a living GRADE guideline, prevention sections first - NPIAP staging unchanged`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Pressure ulcers (pressure injury)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Localised damage to skin and underlying soft tissue over a **bony prominence or under a medical device**, from intense or prolonged **pressure or pressure + shear**. 'Injury' replaced 'ulcer' (NPIAP 2016) because stage 1 and DTPI have intact skin.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**NPIAP**: **stage 1** non-blanchable erythema; **stage 2** partial thickness, dermis exposed; **stage 3** full-thickness skin, fat visible; **stage 4** fascia/muscle/bone exposed; **unstageable** (slough/eschar); **DTPI** (maroon/purple). **Never reverse-stage** ('healing stage 4'). Mucosal injuries not staged.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`**Immobility** (stroke, SCI, dementia, sedation), sensory loss, **moisture/incontinence**, malnutrition, anaemia, hypotension, PAD, diabetes, smoking, aged skin; sites - **sacrum, heels**, trochanters, ischia (sitting), occiput, devices.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pressure over **capillary closing pressure (32 mmHg)** - mattress 70-100 mmHg on sacrum -> ischaemia + **cell deformation** -> damage starts **deep at bone-muscle interface (iceberg/cone)** -> shear (bed head over 30 degrees) and friction -> reperfusion injury -> DTPI evolves over 48-72 h.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`When and how it started (long lie, surgery); hours in one position, who turns; continence; diet and weight loss; pain; comorbidity (stroke, diabetes, PAD); drugs (sedatives, steroids); home pastes applied; fever and smell; **carer capacity**; goals of care; neglect.`],[`Key examination`,`Inspect all pressure points + under devices; **blanch test** (dark skin - warmth, induration, purple hue); measure length x width x depth, **undermining/tunnelling**, photo with ruler; wound bed % tissue types; **probe-to-bone**; infection signs; pulses, **ABPI**; **Braden score**; MNA-SF.`],[`Risk score`,`**Braden 6-23**: **18 or less at risk**, 13-14 moderate, 10-12 high, **9 or less very high**. Re-score on admission, change in condition, at least weekly at home.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Hb, glucose/HbA1c; albumin = illness marker; **deep tissue culture only if clinically infected** (Levine/biopsy, not surface swab); CBC, CRP, **ESR over 70** suggests osteomyelitis; X-ray then **MRI**, **bone biopsy gold standard**; ABPI; **biopsy heaped edge (Marjolin)**.`],[`Treatment - prevention (SSKIN)`,`**Surface** (high-spec foam; air mattress if Braden 12 or less or stage 3-4), **Skin inspection**, **Keep moving** (**2-hourly in bed**, hourly in chair, **30-degree tilt**, bed head 30 degrees or less, **float heels**), **Incontinence** care, **Nutrition 30-35 kcal/kg, protein 1.25-1.5 g/kg**. No donuts, no massage.`],[`Treatment - dressings (TIME)`,`Cleanse with **saline/boiled water** (no routine peroxide, povidone, eusol); **stage 1** film or silicone foam; **stage 2 hydrocolloid** 3-5 days; **exuding stage 3-4 alginate** loosely filled; **foam** secondary; **dry slough - hydrogel**; **collagenase** once daily (not with silver/iodine); leave **stable dry heel eschar**.`],[`Infection and drugs`,`Topical silver/cadexomer iodine **2-week challenge**; **metronidazole gel** for odour; systemic antibiotics only for **cellulitis, osteomyelitis, sepsis**: **co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 5-7 days**; sepsis - **pip-taz 4.5 g IV** or ceftriaxone + metronidazole; osteomyelitis **6 weeks + debridement**; paracetamol before dressings.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Alternating-pressure mattress** - set to weight, inflate 20-30 min, single sheet, **hand check for bottoming out daily**, never switch off; seat cushion with feet supported; **NPWT -125 mmHg**, change 48-72 h, **not over osteomyelitis, malignancy, eschar**; low-cost 4-5 inch high-density foam.`],[`Complications`,`Cellulitis, abscess, **necrotising infection**, **osteomyelitis** (about 1 in 4 non-healing stage 4), septic hip, **sepsis**, protein loss and anaemia, **Marjolin ulcer** (SCC); flaps - gluteal (sacral), TFL (trochanteric), hamstring (ischial); recurrence 30-60%.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Fever, rigors, hypotension, confusion (sepsis - admit); crepitus (necrotising); bone on probing; heel eschar with absent pulses/ABPI under 0.5 (do not debride); heaped edge; no improvement in 2-4 weeks; multiple ulcers with neglect.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Weekly measurements + photo; **PUSH tool** (0-17); stage 2 heals in 2-3 weeks; stage 3-4 should shrink **20-40% in 2-4 weeks** else reassess pressure, infection, nutrition, perfusion; carer fatigue; document every visit.`],[`Special groups`,`**End of life** - comfort goals (odour, pain, fewer turns), warn of **Kennedy terminal ulcer**; **spasticity** - baclofen 5 mg TDS up to 20 mg QDS; **PAD** - check ABPI before debridement; diabetes - control glucose.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**International Guideline 4th edition (2025, living, GRADE)** launched online - prevention first; NPIAP staging unchanged; 2019 3rd edition still the reference for treatment detail.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Off-loading heals, dressings manage. The skin shows the tip of the iceberg. Staging never goes backwards. Braden 18 or less = at risk. No donut rings, no massage. Culture deep tissue, never a surface swab.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Pressure injury at the home visit - assess and act`,steps:[{label:`Fever, rigors, confusion, crepitus?`,detail:`Sepsis or necrotising infection - admit`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Score Braden; inspect all points`,detail:`18 or less at risk; 9 or less very high; check under devices`},{label:`Stage the wound (NPIAP)`,detail:`1, 2, 3, 4, unstageable, DTPI; measure, probe, photograph`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Check perfusion before debriding`,detail:`ABPI under 0.5 or absent pulses - leave dry eschar, refer vascular`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Off-load + SSKIN bundle`,detail:`2-hourly turns, 30-degree tilt, float heels, right surface, nutrition`},{label:`Dress by wound bed (TIME)`,detail:`Hydrocolloid, alginate, foam, hydrogel; review weekly`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Dressing choice by wound bed`,columns:[`Wound bed`,`Dressing`,`Change`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Stage 1 / prevention`,`Film or silicone foam`,`As needed`,`Massage, donut rings`],[`Stage 2, low exudate`,`Hydrocolloid`,`3-5 days (up to 7)`,`Infected or deep wounds, fragile skin`],[`Cavity, heavy exudate`,`Calcium alginate + foam`,`Daily to 2-3 days`,`Dry wounds`],[`Dry slough or necrosis`,`Hydrogel (autolysis) or collagenase`,`1-3 days / daily`,`Collagenase with silver or iodine`],[`Local infection, biofilm`,`Silver or cadexomer iodine`,`2-week challenge`,`Iodine in thyroid disease, pregnancy`],[`Malodorous palliative wound`,`Metronidazole gel + charcoal`,`Once-twice daily 5-7 days`,`Systemic antibiotics for smell alone`],[`Clean deep stage 3-4`,`NPWT -125 mmHg`,`48-72 h`,`Osteomyelitis, malignancy, eschar`]]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-end-of-life-death-certification":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Advance care planning** is an ongoing conversation about values and preferences for future care.`,`An **advance medical directive** is a written refusal of specified treatments; valid in India since 2018.`,`**Withholding and withdrawing** life support are ethically and legally equivalent.`,`**DNAR** means no CPR **and nothing else**; all other care continues.`,`**Palliative sedation** relieves refractory symptoms; the intention is comfort, not death.`,`The **MCCD** is a medical document; the **death certificate** is issued by the Registrar.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Active euthanasia** is a deliberate act to cause death; it is a crime.`,`**Passive euthanasia** is withholding or withdrawing life support; lawful with safeguards.`,`Voluntary is at the patient's request; non-voluntary when the patient cannot express a wish.`,`Involuntary euthanasia, against the expressed wish, is homicide.`,`**Physician-assisted suicide** is abetment of suicide (**BNS 108**).`]},{title:`The Indian legal position`,points:[`There is **no Indian statute**; the law rests on Supreme Court judgments.`,`**Gian Kaur 1996**: Article 21 does not include a right to die.`,`**Aruna Shanbaug 2011** first allowed passive euthanasia, with High Court approval.`,`**Common Cause 2018**: dying with dignity is part of Article 21; advance directives are valid.`,`**24 January 2023**: directive signed before **two witnesses** and attested by a **notary or gazetted officer**.`,`Primary and Secondary Medical Boards each decide **within 48 hours**; the JMFC is informed.`,`**Harish Rana 2026**: tube feeding is medical treatment and may be withdrawn in PVS.`]},{title:`Advance care planning in the OPD`,points:[`Use the **surprise question**: would I be surprised if this patient died within 12 months?`,`Other triggers: CFS 7 or more, repeated admissions, new dementia while capacity remains.`,`Start with permission, then ask what the patient already understands.`,`Ask **what matters most** and what would be unacceptable.`,`Record **specific decisions**: admission, antibiotics, ICU, ventilation, dialysis, tube feeding, CPR.`,`Name who should speak for the patient and review the plan at every change.`]},{title:`Making the decision at the bedside`,points:[`Establish the clinical facts, then check capacity; a capable patient's refusal governs.`,`Hold a family meeting and break news with **SPIKES**.`,`**Do not ask 'do you want us to do everything?'**; recommend what the patient would want.`,`Make it a **team decision** and document it.`,`If the family disagrees, give time, a second opinion or ethics committee; continue full care.`,`**Never act on a request to hasten death**; treat the suffering behind it.`,`Palliative sedation: **midazolam 2.5-5 mg SC** as needed or 10-30 mg over 24 hours.`]},{title:`Death certification (MCCD)`,points:[`Verify death over **at least 5 minutes**: no pulse, heart or breath sounds, fixed pupils.`,`Report deaths within **21 days**, free, under the **RBD Act 1969** as amended 2023.`,`Only the doctor who **attended the last illness** may certify.`,`Part I: immediate cause on line (a), **underlying cause on the lowest line**.`,`Write the interval from onset to death against each line.`,`Part II: other conditions that contributed but are not in the chain.`,`**Never write a mode of dying** such as cardiac arrest or old age as the cause.`]},{title:`Medico-legal deaths and brain-stem death`,points:[`Report every **unnatural or suspicious death** to the police; do not certify it.`,`Deaths during anaesthesia or surgery go to the police with records preserved.`,`A woman's unnatural death **within 7 years of marriage** needs an Executive Magistrate inquest (BNSS 196).`,`**Brain-stem death** under THOTA needs a **board of four**.`,`Do **two sets of tests at least 6 hours apart**, including apnoea.`,`Cornea, skin and bone can be donated after cardiac death.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Contact the bereaved family within a few weeks; **bereavement care** is family medicine.`,`The surviving spouse has excess mortality in the first year: the **widowhood effect**.`,`**Prolonged grief disorder**: beyond 6 months (ICD-11) or 12 months (DSM-5-TR); refer.`,`Audit: documented ACP, DNAR review dates, deaths in preferred place, codable MCCDs.`]}],tables:[{heading:`End-of-life decisions and the law in India`,columns:[`Term`,`Meaning`,`Legal position`],rows:[[`Withholding or withdrawing`,`Not starting or stopping life support`,`Lawful; two-board process (2023)`],[`Advance medical directive`,`Written refusal of specified future treatment`,`Valid; 2 witnesses plus notary`],[`DNAR`,`No CPR`,`Lawful; all other care continues`],[`Palliative sedation`,`Sedation for refractory symptoms`,`Lawful; double effect`],[`Active euthanasia`,`Deliberate act such as lethal injection`,`Crime (BNS 103, 105)`],[`Assisted suicide`,`Doctor provides means`,`Crime (BNS 108)`]]},{heading:`MCCD Form 4 - worked example`,columns:[`Part`,`What goes there`,`Example`,`Interval`],rows:[[`I (a)`,`Immediate cause`,`Aspiration pneumonia`,`3 days`],[`I (b)`,`Condition causing (a)`,`Dysphagia`,`4 months`],[`I (c)`,`Underlying cause, coded`,`Cerebral infarction`,`6 months`],[`Part II`,`Contributing, not in chain`,`Type 2 diabetes, hypertension`,`-`],[`Never alone`,`Modes of dying`,`Cardiac arrest, shock, old age`,`-`]]},{heading:`Deaths needing police intimation`,columns:[`Category`,`Examples`,`Action`],rows:[[`Injury and accident`,`Road accident, burns, drowning`,`Treat first, MLC, police, autopsy`],[`Poisoning`,`Overdose, suspected poisoning`,`Preserve samples; inform police`],[`Suicide or homicide`,`Hanging, suspected homicide`,`Do not certify; autopsy`],[`Custodial`,`Police or judicial custody`,`Magisterial inquiry`],[`Procedure-related`,`During anaesthesia or surgery`,`Preserve records; autopsy`],[`Woman within 7 years of marriage`,`Unnatural death`,`Executive Magistrate inquest`]]},{heading:`Key cases and dates`,columns:[`Case`,`Year`,`Holding`],rows:[[`Gian Kaur`,`1996`,`No right to die under Article 21`],[`Aruna Shanbaug`,`2011`,`Passive euthanasia with High Court approval`],[`Common Cause`,`2018`,`Dignified death in Article 21; directives valid`],[`Common Cause modification`,`24 Jan 2023`,`Notary, two boards in 48 h, JMFC informed`],[`Harish Rana`,`11 Mar 2026`,`Tube feeding may be withdrawn in PVS`]]}],remember:[`Surprise question starts ACP.`,`DNAR is about CPR only.`,`Withholding equals withdrawing; offer time-limited trials.`,`SPIKES: Setting, Perception, Invitation, Knowledge, Emotions, Strategy.`,`Underlying cause goes on the lowest line of Part I.`,`Cardiac arrest is a mode of dying, not a cause.`,`Doctors issue the MCCD; the Registrar issues the death certificate.`,`Brain-stem death: board of four, two tests 6 hours apart.`]},updated:[`Harish Rana v Union of India (SC, 11 March 2026): first application of Common Cause - clinically assisted nutrition and hydration is medical treatment that may be withdrawn in PVS; states to keep CMO panels for Secondary Medical Boards`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - End-of-life decisions, ACP and death certification`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Advance care planning** - ongoing conversation about values, goals and future care preferences while capacity exists. **Limitation of life-sustaining treatment** = withholding or withdrawing (ethically equivalent). **DNAR** = no CPR only - all other care continues.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Euthanasia: **active** (crime) vs **passive** (lawful with safeguards); **voluntary, non-voluntary, involuntary** (homicide); **physician-assisted suicide** - abetment (**BNS 108**); **palliative sedation** - lawful, double effect.`],[`Legal history`,`**Gian Kaur 1996** - no right to die in Art 21; **Aruna Shanbaug 2011** - passive euthanasia via High Court; **Common Cause 2018** - right to die with dignity, living will valid; **modified 24 January 2023**; **Harish Rana 2026** - first application, CANH withdrawable.`],[`Advance directive (2023)`,`Signed by executor before **2 witnesses**, attested by **notary or gazetted officer** (no magistrate); copies to guardians, family physician, local-government custodian; may be linked to digital health record.`],[`Withdrawal process (2023)`,`**Primary Medical Board** (treating doctor + 2 experts of 5 years) -> **Secondary Medical Board** (CMO/DHO nominee + 2 experts) - **each decides within 48 h**, with surrogate consent; **JMFC informed, not asked to approve**; refusal -> High Court (Art 226). Harish Rana 2026: **CMO panels** for secondary boards.`],[`Key history (ACP)`,`Triggers - **surprise question**, new incurable diagnosis, **CFS 7+**, second unplanned admission, early dementia; ask permission and perception; **what matters most, what is unacceptable**; who should speak for them; record **specific decisions** (admission, ICU, ventilation, dialysis, tube feeding, CPR).`],[`Bedside decision steps`,`Clinical facts and prognosis -> **capacity** (capacitous refusal governs) -> **family meeting**, SPIKES, no euphemism -> **recommend, do not ask 'do everything?'** -> team decision, documented -> disagreement: time, second opinion, ethics committee, full care meanwhile.`],[`Palliative sedation`,`Refractory symptoms in last days - **midazolam 2.5-5 mg SC** PRN or **10-30 mg/24 h** by driver, titrated to comfort; record indication and intention; **double effect** - titrated opioids do not shorten life.`],[`Death verification`,`Observe **5 minutes**: no central pulse or heart sounds, no breath sounds or effort, **fixed dilated pupils**, no response to supraorbital pressure; record date, time, name.`],[`Death registration (RBD Act)`,`**RBD Act 1969, amended 2023** (from **1 Oct 2023**) - **digital registration**, national database; report within **21 days** free; after 1 year - DM/SDM order; institutions send **MCCD to Registrar + copy to relative**; MCCD free of charge.`],[`MCCD rules`,`**Form 4** (institutional) / **4A** (non-institutional); **Part I** sequence - (a) immediate, (b), (c) - **underlying cause on lowest completed line** (coded); intervals lengthen downward; **Part II** contributing conditions; **never a mode of dying** (cardiac arrest, respiratory failure) or 'old age' alone; no abbreviations.`],[`Medico-legal deaths`,`Police for **unnatural, suspicious, custodial, procedure-related, unknown cause**; woman within **7 years of marriage** - Executive Magistrate inquest (**BNSS 196**); **do not certify** unnatural deaths; Good Samaritan protection (MV Act s134A).`],[`Brain-stem death`,`**THOTA 1994/2011, Rules 2014** - **board of 4** (RMP in charge, panel RMP, neurologist/neurosurgeon, treating doctor); **2 sets of tests 6 h apart** incl. apnoea test; legal time of death; donation after declaration; cornea, skin, bone after cardiac death.`],[`Bereavement`,`Inform in person; contact within weeks; **widowhood effect**; **prolonged grief disorder** - beyond **6 months (ICD-11) / 12 months (DSM-5-TR)** - refer; screen depression, suicide.`],[`Red flags`,`DNAR read as 'do not treat'; request for lethal dose; certifying an unnatural death; 'cardiorespiratory arrest' as cause; withdrawal without board process; forcing futile treatment; bereaved spouse not eating months later.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Harish Rana v UoI (11 March 2026)** - CANH is treatment, withdrawable in PVS with boards and palliative care; MoHFW 2024 withdrawal guidelines still draft; Common Cause 2023 procedure governs.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Surprise question starts ACP. Withholding = withdrawing. Two witnesses + notary; two boards in 48 h; JMFC informed. Underlying cause on lowest line of Part I. Doctors issue MCCD, Registrar issues death certificate.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment in India (2023, as applied 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Patient has capacity?`,detail:`Yes - informed refusal governs; record it`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No capacity - valid advance directive?`,detail:`Two witnesses + notary/gazetted officer; follow it`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Primary Medical Board within 48 h`,detail:`Treating doctor + 2 experts of 5 years; surrogate consents`},{label:`Secondary Medical Board within 48 h`,detail:`CMO/DHO nominee from panel + 2 experts`},{label:`Inform JMFC; withdraw with palliative care`,detail:`CANH may be withdrawn (Harish Rana 2026); comfort care continues`,tone:`good`},{label:`Board refuses - High Court (Art 226)`,detail:`Full care continues meanwhile`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Writing the MCCD - Form 4`,steps:[{label:`Unnatural or unknown cause?`,detail:`Do not certify - police, autopsy`,tone:`warn`},{label:`I(a) immediate cause`,detail:`A disease, not a mode of dying (e.g. aspiration pneumonia - 3 days)`},{label:`I(b), I(c) antecedent causes`,detail:`Dysphagia - 4 months; cerebral infarction - 6 months`},{label:`Underlying cause on lowest completed line`,detail:`This is coded for national statistics`,tone:`good`},{label:`Part II contributing conditions`,detail:`Diabetes, hypertension; pregnancy question in women`},{label:`Send to Registrar, copy to relative`,detail:`Free of charge; registration within 21 days (RBD 2023)`}]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-elderly-community-health":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`An **elderly person** in India is anyone **60 years and above**.`,`The elderly were **8.6% in Census 2011**, about 10.5% in 2022.`,`They will reach **20.8% (347 million) by 2050**.`,`**Healthy ageing** (WHO) means maintaining functional ability for well-being.`,`Functional ability is **intrinsic capacity** interacting with the environment.`,`**LASI Wave 1** (2017-18) is India's largest ageing survey.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Age bands: **young-old 60-69**, old-old 70-79, **oldest-old 80 and above**.`,`The oldest-old grow fastest and carry most disability.`,`Ageing in India is **feminised, ruralised** and increasingly lived alone.`,`Over half of women 60 and above are widowed.`,`About 70% of the elderly live in rural areas.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Falling fertility and mortality drive the **demographic transition**.`,`Joint families break up as children migrate for work.`,`Three-quarters have a chronic disease; one-quarter have **multimorbidity**.`,`Only a minority have a pension; health spending is often catastrophic.`,`The **geriatric giants**: immobility, instability, incontinence, intellectual impairment, iatrogenesis.`,`Sensory loss is the commonest and most treatable disability.`,`Elderly men living alone have the **highest suicide risk**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Screen the six **ICOPE domains**: cognition, mobility, nutrition, vision, hearing, mood.`,`Take a function history first: **Katz ADL** and **Lawton IADL**.`,`Any recent loss of function is disease, not age.`,`Ask about falls, living arrangements, pension, insurance and the caregiver's health.`,`**Interview the elder alone** and ask about abuse.`,`Use the **Elder Abuse Suspicion Index**; one positive on questions 2-6 raises suspicion.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Time the chair rise (5 rises **under 14 seconds**) or Timed Up and Go.`,`**TUG 12 seconds or more** means high fall risk.`,`Screen cognition with Mini-Cog or adapted MMSE, and mood with **GDS-15** (5 or more).`,`Record weight, calf circumference and MNA-SF.`,`Check vision with a Snellen chart and hearing with a whispered voice.`,`Look for patterned bruises, poor hygiene, dehydration and pressure ulcers.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Activity: **150-300 minutes a week** plus balance and strength on 3 or more days.`,`Protein **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day**, calcium 1000-1200 mg, salt under 5 g.`,`Vaccinate: yearly influenza, pneumococcal, Td every 10 years, recombinant zoster.`,`Make the home safe: **night lights, grab bars**, non-slip floors, handrails.`,`Display emergency numbers **108 and 14567** by the phone.`,`Offer pre-retirement counselling and a health check.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`WHO: about **1 in 6** people 60 and above suffer abuse each year.`,`Types: physical, psychological, **financial**, sexual, **neglect**, abandonment.`,`Risk factors: dependency, dementia, caregiver stress and abuser dependence on the elder.`,`Document injuries with body diagrams and photographs.`,`Physical or sexual abuse is an offence under BNS; inform police and register an MLC.`,`World Elder Abuse Awareness Day is **15 June**; Older Persons Day is **1 October**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**MWPSC Act 2007**: tribunal can order up to **Rs 10,000 a month** within 90 days.`,`Abandonment is punishable with **3 months or Rs 5000** fine.`,`**NPHCE 2010**: care from Ayushman Arogya Mandir to two National Centres of Ageing.`,`District hospitals have a **10-bed geriatric ward**.`,`**PM-JAY Vay Vandana** (Oct 2024): Rs 5 lakh a year for all 70 and above, any income.`,`**IGNOAPS**: Rs 200 a month at 60-79 and Rs 500 from 80, for BPL.`,`**Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana** gives free aids; **Elderline 14567** is the helpline.`]},{title:`Disability and the RPwD Act`,points:[`The **RPwD Act 2016** recognises **21 disabilities**.`,`**Benchmark disability is 40% or more**.`,`It gives **4% reservation in government jobs** and 5% in higher education.`,`Certificates are linked to the **UDID card**.`,`WHO CBR matrix: health, education, livelihood, social, empowerment.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Keep an **elderly register** with flags: living alone, frailty, dementia, falls, polypharmacy.`,`Offer an annual review with ICOPE screening and medication reconciliation.`,`Make home visits for the bedbound and screen the caregiver for burnout.`,`Refer complex cases to the district geriatric clinic or Regional Geriatric Centre.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Vaccines for older adults`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Influenza (inactivated)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Every year`,`Sore arm, low fever 1-2 days`],[`PCV20 or PCV21`,`0.5 mL IM once`,`From age 50; completes series`,`Local pain, myalgia`],[`PCV15 then PPSV23`,`0.5 mL IM each`,`PPSV23 at least 1 year later`,`PPSV23 only after conjugate`],[`RSV vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM single dose`,`75+, or 50-74 if at risk`,`Rare Guillain-Barre syndrome`],[`Td`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Every 10 years`,`Arthus reaction if too frequent`],[`Recombinant zoster`,`0.5 mL IM, 2 doses`,`2-6 months apart, from 50`,`Pain, fever 1-3 days; safe if immunocompromised`]]},{heading:`Types of elder abuse`,columns:[`Type`,`Examples`,`Warning signs`],rows:[[`Physical`,`Hitting, restraint, over-sedation`,`Unexplained bruises, fractures, burns`],[`Psychological`,`Insults, threats, isolation`,`Fear, withdrawal, depression`],[`Financial`,`Taking pension, forced property transfer`,`Unpaid bills, sudden asset transfer`],[`Sexual`,`Non-consensual contact`,`Genital injury, STI`],[`Neglect`,`Withholding food, medicine, hygiene`,`Malnutrition, pressure ulcers, dehydration`],[`Abandonment`,`Deserted at hospital or pilgrimage site`,`Elder alone without means`]]},{heading:`Laws and schemes for senior citizens`,columns:[`Measure`,`Purpose`,`Key number`],rows:[[`MWPSC Act 2007`,`Legal protection`,`Rs 10,000 a month; 90 days`],[`NPHCE 2010`,`Health care`,`10-bed district geriatric ward`],[`PM-JAY Vay Vandana 2024`,`Health insurance`,`Rs 5 lakh a year, all 70+`],[`IGNOAPS`,`Pension`,`Rs 200 (60-79), Rs 500 (80+)`],[`Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana`,`Free aids`,`Spectacles, hearing aids, dentures`],[`Elderline`,`Helpline`,`14567`],[`RPwD Act 2016`,`Disability rights`,`21 disabilities, benchmark 40%`]]}],remember:[`India's elderly: 8.6% in 2011, about 20.8% by 2050.`,`ICOPE six: cognition, mobility, nutrition, vision, hearing, mood.`,`MWPSC: Rs 10,000, 90 days, abandonment punishable.`,`PM-JAY covers everyone 70 and above, regardless of income.`,`Elderline is 14567 - quote it.`,`Always interview a suspected abused elder alone.`,`RPwD: 21 disabilities, benchmark 40%, UDID card.`,`A grab bar and a night light prevent more fractures than most drugs.`]},updated:[`ACIP 2024-25: pneumococcal vaccination from age 50 - PCV20 or PCV21 alone, or PCV15 followed by PPSV23 after 1 year (PCV13-based schedule retired)`,`ACIP 2025: RSV vaccine added - single dose at 75 and above and at 50-74 with increased risk`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Health of the elderly, elder abuse and social support`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Senior citizen = 60+** (NPOP 1999, MWPSC 2007); WHO 60 (LMIC) / 65 (HIC). **Healthy ageing** = maintaining **functional ability** (intrinsic capacity x environment). **Elder abuse** = act or omission in a relationship of **trust** causing harm.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Young-old 60-69, old-old 70-79, oldest-old 80+** (fastest growing); old-age dependency ratio (60+ per 100 aged 15-59); abuse types - physical, psychological, **financial**, sexual, **neglect**, abandonment, self-neglect.`],[`Demography (India)`,`**8.6% (104 million) 2011 -> 10.5% (149 million) 2022 -> 20.8% (347 million) by 2050**; **feminisation** (over half of older women widowed), **ruralisation** (about 70%), living alone; LASI Wave 1 (72,000 aged 45+); life expectancy at 60 about 18 years.`],[`Problems of the elderly`,`**NCDs** (hypertension about 1/3), **geriatric giants** + iatrogenesis, **sensory loss** (commonest disability), osteoporosis, malnutrition, oral disease; **depression 1 in 5**, **dementia 7-8%** (LASI-DAD); isolation, retirement, economic dependence, out-of-pocket spending.`],[`Assessment`,`**WHO ICOPE 6 domains** - cognition, mobility (**5 chair rises under 14 s**), nutrition (**weight loss over 3 kg in 3 months**), vision, hearing, mood; ADL (Katz) and IADL (Lawton); GDS-15; **EASI** for abuse - interview alone; CGA yearly or after decline.`],[`Healthy ageing measures`,`**Activity 150-300 min/week + strength/balance 3+ days**; protein 1.0-1.2 g/kg; salt under 5 g; stop tobacco; social engagement; pre-retirement counselling; **elder-friendly home** (grab bars, night lights, ramps 1:12); emergency numbers **108 and 14567**.`],[`Vaccines`,`**Influenza yearly**; **pneumococcal from 50 - PCV20/PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23 after 1 year**; **Td every 10 years**; **recombinant zoster 2 doses 2-6 months apart from 50**; **RSV single dose 75+ (50-74 at risk)**.`],[`Screening`,`BP, glucose, lipids; oral cavity; breast; cervix to 65; colorectal as indicated; **vision and hearing yearly**; bone health in women 65+; falls; cognition; depression.`],[`Elder abuse - recognition`,`WHO **1 in 6** 60+ yearly; LASI about 5% self-reported (sons, daughters-in-law); signs - patterned bruises, **delay in care**, poor hygiene, malnutrition, **pressure ulcers**, fear of carer, sudden financial change; risk - dependency, dementia, **carer stress**, abuser dependent on elder.`],[`Elder abuse - response`,`Interview **alone**, assess capacity and safety, **document with body diagrams/photos**, treat, support carer, respite; **Elderline 14567**, police senior cells, **Maintenance Tribunal**, legal aid; physical/sexual abuse - MLC and police (BNS).`],[`MWPSC Act 2007`,`Children and inheriting relatives must maintain; **Tribunal (SDM) up to Rs 10,000/month, decide in 90 days**; **abandonment - 3 months or Rs 5000 or both**; property transferred on condition of care **voidable (s23)**; one old-age home per district.`],[`NPHCE (2010)`,`**Ayushman Arogya Mandir** screening and home care -> PHC weekly clinic -> **CHC clinic + rehabilitation** -> **district 10-bed ward** -> **Regional Geriatric Centres** -> **National Centres of Ageing (AIIMS Delhi, MMC Chennai)**.`],[`Social security schemes`,`**PM-JAY Vay Vandana (29 Oct 2024)** - all **70+**, Rs 5 lakh/year regardless of income; **IGNOAPS Rs 200 (60-79), Rs 500 (80+)** BPL; **AVYAY** umbrella; **Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana** free aids; SCSS; CGHS/ECHS/ESIC; UN Decade of Healthy Ageing 2021-2030.`],[`Disability (RPwD Act 2016)`,`**21 disabilities**; **benchmark 40%** - **4% jobs, 5% higher education** reservation; certificate by medical board + **UDID card**; Sugamya Bharat; **CBR matrix** - health, education, livelihood, social, empowerment.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Unexplained injuries with delay; acute confusion (delirium); recurrent falls/long lie; weight loss over 5%; **suicidal elderly man living alone**; neglect signs; abandoned elder - Elderline, police, tribunal.`],[`Family physician's role`,`**Elderly register** with risk flags; annual ICOPE review, medication reconciliation, vaccines, ACP; home visits for bedbound; carer screening; **link to entitlements** (Vay Vandana card, pensions, Vayoshri, UDID); refer to district geriatric clinic.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ACIP 2024-25**: pneumococcal from 50 with PCV20/PCV21 (PCV13 schedule retired) and **RSV vaccine** at 75+ and 50-74 at risk; PM-JAY 70+ expansion (2024) and UNFPA India Ageing Report 2023 remain the current Indian anchors.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Elderly double from 10% to over 20% by 2050. ICOPE screens six domains. MWPSC: Rs 10,000, 90 days, abandonment punishable. Elderline 14567. Always interview a suspected abused elder alone. 15 June - Elder Abuse Awareness Day; 1 October - Older Persons Day.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected elder abuse - family physician response`,steps:[{label:`Warning signs present?`,detail:`Delay in care, patterned bruises, neglect, pressure ulcers, fearful of carer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Interview the elder alone`,detail:`EASI questions; assess capacity`},{label:`Immediate danger?`,detail:`Yes - safe place, police, MLC if physical or sexual abuse`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Document and treat`,detail:`Body diagram, photographs, objective account`},{label:`Support and remedies`,detail:`Carer respite, Elderline 14567, Maintenance Tribunal (Rs 10,000/month, 90 days), legal aid`,tone:`good`},{label:`Follow up`,detail:`Review at short intervals; involve social worker and NPHCE team`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`NPHCE - levels of geriatric care`,steps:[{label:`Ayushman Arogya Mandir (SHC)`,detail:`Screening, home visits, bedbound care by CHO and ASHA`},{label:`PHC`,detail:`Weekly geriatric clinic`},{label:`CHC`,detail:`Geriatric clinic and rehabilitation unit`},{label:`District hospital`,detail:`Daily clinic and 10-bed geriatric ward`},{label:`Regional Geriatric Centre`,detail:`Medical college: specialist care, training`},{label:`National Centres of Ageing`,detail:`AIIMS New Delhi and Madras Medical College, Chennai`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Vaccines for older adults - schedule to quote`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Who and when`,`Note`],rows:[[`Influenza (inactivated)`,`Every year, all 60+`,`Before monsoon or winter peak`],[`Pneumococcal conjugate`,`From 50: PCV20 or PCV21 once`,`Or PCV15 then PPSV23 after 1 year`],[`Recombinant zoster`,`50+, 2 doses 2-6 months apart`,`Safe in immunocompromised; give after past zoster`],[`Td`,`Every 10 years; 3 doses if never immunised`,`Booster after dirty wound if over 5 years`],[`RSV (prefusion F)`,`Single dose at 75+; 50-74 if at increased risk`,`No booster; give before RSV season`]]}]},"geriatrics-ethics-medicolegal-negligence-ethics":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Medical negligence** is failing to use the reasonable skill and care of an ordinary competent doctor.`,`Neither the highest skill nor a guaranteed result is required.`,`**Four Ds**: Duty, Dereliction, Direct causation, Damage; all must be proved.`,`A bona fide **error of judgement** or a rare drug reaction is not negligence.`,`Since **1 July 2024**, BNS, BNSS and BSA replace the IPC, CrPC and Evidence Act.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Civil** negligence: compensation, on the balance of probabilities.`,`**Criminal** negligence: punishment, beyond reasonable doubt, and must be **gross**.`,`**Professional misconduct**: licence action by the medical council; no harm needed.`,`One act can lead to all three.`,`**Contributory negligence** by the patient reduces compensation.`,`**Vicarious liability** makes the hospital answerable for its staff.`]},{title:`Ethical principles`,points:[`Four principles: **autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice**.`,`None automatically overrides another; weigh them in each case.`,`Autonomy is exercised through **informed consent**; a competent adult may refuse.`,`Non-maleficence: no antibiotics for viral URI, no long-term benzodiazepines in the old.`,`Justice: fair use of scarce resources regardless of caste, gender or payment.`,`Confidentiality yields to law or to **serious risk to an identified person**.`]},{title:`Civil negligence - standard and cases`,points:[`**Bolam**: not negligent if a responsible body of doctors would act the same way.`,`**Bolitho**: that body of opinion must withstand **logical analysis**.`,`**Kusum Sharma 2010**: choosing one of two accepted methods is not negligence.`,`**Res ipsa loquitur**: a swab left inside or wrong-side surgery shifts the burden to the doctor.`,`**Balram Prasad v Kunal Saha 2013**: about **Rs 6 crore** compensation.`,`Commonest primary-care finding: **delay or failure to refer**.`,`File civil suits within **3 years**; consumer complaints within **2 years**.`]},{title:`Criminal law for doctors`,points:[`**BNS 106(1)** (old IPC 304A): death by negligence, up to 5 years.`,`For a registered doctor during a procedure, the limit is **up to 2 years** and fine.`,`**Jacob Mathew 2005**: needs gross negligence and an **independent medical opinion**.`,`Under Jacob Mathew, doctors should **not be arrested routinely**.`,`Grievous hurt (**BNS 116**) now uses **15 days**, not 20.`,`A false certificate is an offence under **BNS 234** (old IPC 197).`,`**BNSS 397**: every hospital must treat rape and acid-attack victims free.`]},{title:`Consumer law and the NMC`,points:[`**V.P. Shantha 1995**: paid medical services fall under consumer law.`,`In **November 2024** the larger-bench reference was declined; V.P. Shantha still binds.`,`Jurisdiction by fee paid: District up to **Rs 50 lakh**, State to Rs 2 crore, National above.`,`**V. Kishan Rao 2010**: expert opinion is not needed in every consumer case.`,`NMC 2023 conduct regulations are **in abeyance**; IMC 2002 regulations still apply.`,`Prescribe by **generic name**, legibly, with registration number; accept no industry gifts.`,`Complaints go to the State Medical Council, with appeal to the NMC **EMRB**.`]},{title:`The medico-legal case`,points:[`Register an MLC for injury, burns, poisoning, assault, sexual assault and unexplained unconsciousness.`,`**Treat first, paperwork later** (Parmanand Katara 1989).`,`**Good Samaritans** cannot be detained or forced to be witnesses (MV Act 134A).`,`Inform police in writing and record the officer's name and time.`,`Describe each injury objectively and keep the **chain of custody**.`,`**POCSO Section 19**: any doctor must report suspected child sexual abuse.`,`Never certify an unnatural death; it goes to the police and autopsy.`]},{title:`Research ethics`,points:[`Trials follow the **NDCT Rules 2019** with CDSCO as licensing authority.`,`Every trial needs an Ethics Committee and **CTRI registration** before enrolment.`,`Report **serious adverse events within 24 hours**.`,`Compensation for trial injury is mandatory; vulnerable participants need audio-visual consent.`,`**ICMR National Ethical Guidelines 2017** set the principles.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Most disputes arise from **poor communication**, unmet expectations and cost.`,`**Contemporaneous notes, consent and timely written referral** are the best defence.`,`Never alter records after an adverse event; add a dated addendum.`,`Hold **professional indemnity insurance**.`,`Violence during epidemics: **3 months to 5 years** under the Epidemic Diseases Amendment 2020.`,`After violence, the institution must file an **FIR within 6 hours**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of liability`,columns:[`Type`,`Forum`,`Standard of proof`,`Outcome`],rows:[[`Civil`,`Civil court or Consumer Commission`,`Balance of probabilities; Bolam-Bolitho`,`Compensation`],[`Criminal`,`Criminal court, BNS 106(1)`,`Beyond reasonable doubt; gross negligence`,`Up to 2 years for a doctor`],[`Misconduct`,`State Medical Council, NMC EMRB`,`Breach of conduct regulations`,`Warning to removal from register`]]},{heading:`Old and new sections for doctors`,columns:[`Subject`,`Old law`,`New law`],rows:[[`Death by negligence`,`IPC 304A`,`BNS 106(1)`],[`Good-faith act with consent`,`IPC 88`,`BNS 26`],[`Emergency without consent`,`IPC 92`,`BNS 30`],[`Grievous hurt`,`IPC 320 (20 days)`,`BNS 116 (15 days)`],[`False certificate`,`IPC 197`,`BNS 234`],[`FIR`,`CrPC 154`,`BNSS 173`],[`Free treatment of rape or acid victims`,`CrPC 357C`,`BNSS 397`],[`Expert opinion; dying declaration`,`IEA 45, 32`,`BSA 39, 26`],[`Electronic records`,`IEA 65B`,`BSA 63`]]},{heading:`Landmark judgments`,columns:[`Case`,`Year`,`Holding`],rows:[[`Parmanand Katara`,`1989`,`Emergency care first`],[`IMA v V.P. Shantha`,`1995`,`Paid medical care under consumer law`],[`Jacob Mathew`,`2005`,`Criminal negligence must be gross; no routine arrest`],[`Samira Kohli`,`2008`,`Consent is procedure-specific`],[`Kusum Sharma`,`2010`,`Error of judgement is not negligence`],[`V. Kishan Rao`,`2010`,`Expert opinion not always needed`],[`Balram Prasad v Kunal Saha`,`2013`,`About Rs 6 crore compensation`]]}],remember:[`Four Ds: Duty, Dereliction, Direct causation, Damage.`,`Civil uses Bolam plus Bolitho; criminal needs gross negligence (Jacob Mathew).`,`BNS 106(1): up to 2 years for a doctor during a procedure.`,`Grievous hurt dropped from 20 to 15 days.`,`Consumer: Rs 50 lakh, Rs 2 crore; file within 2 years.`,`Treat first, inform police next.`,`NMC 2023 in abeyance: quote IMC 2002 too.`,`Good notes and timely written referral are the best indemnity.`]},updated:[`Supreme Court 7 November 2024: three-judge bench declined the D.K. Gandhi reference on V.P. Shantha as unnecessary - medical services remain under the Consumer Protection Act (a fresh healthcare providers' petition is pending)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Medical ethics, negligence and the medico-legal case`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Medical negligence** = breach of the duty to use the **reasonable skill and care of an ordinary competent practitioner**, directly causing damage; not the highest skill, not a guaranteed result. **Four Ds**: Duty, Dereliction, Direct causation, Damage.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Civil** (compensation, balance of probabilities, Bolam-Bolitho) vs **criminal** (beyond reasonable doubt, **gross negligence**, BNS 106(1)) vs **professional misconduct** (medical council, no harm needed). Also contributory negligence, vicarious liability, res ipsa loquitur.`],[`Ethical principles`,`**Autonomy** (informed consent, refusal), **beneficence**, **non-maleficence**, **justice** (Beauchamp and Childress) - prima facie, weighed case by case; confidentiality with defined exceptions; good-faith acts protected by **BNS 26, 27, 30, 31**.`],[`Standard of care`,`**Bolam** - practice accepted by a responsible body of opinion; **Bolitho** - opinion must withstand logical analysis; **Kusum Sharma 2010** - error of judgement or choosing one of two accepted methods is not negligence; **res ipsa loquitur** - swab left, wrong-side surgery.`],[`Key civil cases`,`**Spring Meadows 1998** - hospital vicariously liable; **Balram Prasad v Kunal Saha 2013** - about Rs 6 crore; **V. Kishan Rao 2010** - expert opinion not mandatory in every consumer case; limitation **civil 3 years, consumer 2 years**.`],[`Criminal law (BNS/BNSS/BSA)`,`From **1 July 2024**: **BNS 106(1)** (IPC 304A) - up to 5 years; **RMP proviso up to 2 years** + fine; **Jacob Mathew 2005** - gross negligence, prior credible medical opinion, no routine arrest; **BNS 116** grievous hurt **15 days** (was 20); **BNS 234** false certificate.`],[`Procedure sections`,`**BNSS 173** FIR (zero/e-FIR); **BNSS 194/196** inquests; **BNSS 184** rape survivor exam with consent within 24 h, report in 7 days; **BNSS 397** free treatment of rape/acid victims; **BSA 39** expert opinion; **BSA 26** dying declaration; **BSA 63** electronic records.`],[`Consumer Protection Act 2019`,`**V.P. Shantha 1995** - paid services covered, free-to-all excluded; **D.K. Gandhi 2024 reference declined 7 Nov 2024 - Shantha stands**; jurisdiction by consideration paid - **District up to Rs 50 lakh, State Rs 50 lakh-2 crore, National above**; file within **2 years**; e-Daakhil; mediation.`],[`Professional regulation`,`**IMC Regulations 2002 govern** - NMC RMP Regulations 2023 **in abeyance**; generic legible prescribing with registration number; **no gifts or travel from industry (UCPMP 2024)**; records 3 years, copies in 72 h; no testimonials or before-after photos; complaints - SMC -> **NMC EMRB**.`],[`The MLC`,`Register for injury, RTA, burns, poisoning, assault, sexual assault, unconscious of unknown cause, brought dead, custodial; **treat first (Parmanand Katara 1989)**; Good Samaritans protected (MV Act s134A); written police intimation; objective injury documentation; chain of custody.`],[`Certificates and records`,`Sickness/fitness certificate only after personal examination; **MCCD** - underlying cause lowest line, never an unnatural death; **POCSO s19** mandatory reporting, **s21** failure is an offence; contemporaneous notes, **never alter - dated addendum**; LAMA form with witness.`],[`Research ethics`,`**NDCT Rules 2019** - CDSCO, registered Ethics Committee, **CTRI** registration before enrolment; **SAE within 24 h**; compensation for injury; **audio-visual consent** for vulnerable participants; **ICMR 2017** principles.`],[`Prevention`,`Communication, consent with counselling note, **timely written referral** (delayed referral is commonest finding), good records, **indemnity insurance**; avoid defensive medicine.`],[`Violence against doctors`,`**Epidemic Diseases (Amendment) Act 2020** - 3 months-5 years (6 months-7 years if grievous); state Medicare Acts; after 2024 Kolkata incident - National Task Force, **institutional FIR within 6 hours**; clinic security and de-escalation.`],[`Red flags`,`Injury or poisoning without MLC; suspected child sexual abuse (POCSO); deterioration beyond facility; request to alter notes; LAMA; certifying unseen or unnatural death; aggressive relatives; trial SAE.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**V.P. Shantha reference declined (7 Nov 2024)** - doctors stay under CPA 2019; BNS/BNSS/BSA in force since 1 July 2024; NMC 2023 regulations still in abeyance (IMC 2002 applies).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Four Ds. Bolam with Bolitho for civil, Jacob Mathew for criminal. BNS 106(1) - 2 years for an RMP. Grievous hurt now 15 days. Treat first, inform police next. Good notes and timely referral are the best indemnity.`]]},flows:[{kind:`branch`,heading:`One act, three liabilities`,root:`Alleged medical negligence`,arms:[{label:`Civil`,steps:[`Consumer Commission or civil court`,`Bolam-Bolitho; balance of probabilities`,`Compensation; 2-year consumer limitation`]},{label:`Criminal`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`BNS 106(1) (old IPC 304A)`,`Gross negligence; Jacob Mathew safeguards`,`Up to 2 years for an RMP`]},{label:`Disciplinary`,steps:[`State Medical Council, appeal NMC EMRB`,`IMC 2002 regulations (NMC 2023 in abeyance)`,`Warning to removal from register`]}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Handling a medico-legal case in the clinic`,steps:[{label:`Stabilise and treat first`,detail:`Parmanand Katara 1989 - no waiting for police`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Is it an MLC?`,detail:`Injury, RTA, burns, poisoning, assault, sexual assault, unknown unconsciousness`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Consent and history in patient's words`,detail:`Sexual assault: BNSS 184 with consent; child: POCSO s19 report`},{label:`Document injuries objectively`,detail:`Type, site, size, age; simple or grievous (BNS 116, 15 days); seal samples`},{label:`Inform police in writing`,detail:`Name of officer, time, acknowledgement; MLC register`},{label:`Refer in writing if beyond capability`,detail:`Copy of notes; never alter records later`,tone:`good`}]}]}};export{e as default};